<hr/>
<h2 id="p1-1-oop-language-fundamentals">1. OOP &amp; Language Fundamentals<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p1-1-oop-language-fundamentals">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q1-1"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q1</span><span class="qtext">Difference between abstract class and interface (Java 8+)?</span></p><div class="a"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>Abstract class</th>
<th>Interface</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>State</td>
<td>Can have instance fields</td>
<td>Only <code>public static final</code> constants</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Constructor</td>
<td>Yes</td>
<td>No</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Methods</td>
<td>abstract, concrete, private, static</td>
<td>abstract, <code>default</code>, <code>static</code>, <code>private</code> (Java 9+)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Inheritance</td>
<td>Single</td>
<td>Multiple</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Access modifiers</td>
<td>any</td>
<td>methods implicitly <code>public</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Use when</td>
<td>"is-a" with shared state/partial implementation</td>
<td>capability/contract, multiple inheritance of type</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table><p>Since Java 8, interfaces got <code>default</code> methods so they can carry behaviour — but never <em>state</em>. That's the real remaining distinction. Use an abstract class when subclasses must share fields and a constructor lifecycle; use an interface for capability contracts (<code>Comparable</code>, <code>Serializable</code>, <code>PaymentProcessor</code>).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-2"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q2</span><span class="qtext">What happens if a class implements two interfaces with the same default method?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Compile error — "inherits unrelated defaults". You must override it and can disambiguate with <code>InterfaceA.super.method()</code>.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">interface</span> <span class="nc">A</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">default</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">hi</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"A"</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="kd">interface</span> <span class="nc">B</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">default</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">hi</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"B"</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">C</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">implements</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">A</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">B</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">hi</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">A</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">super</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">hi</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// required</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-3"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q3</span><span class="qtext">Method overloading vs overriding.</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li>Overloading = compile-time (static) polymorphism, same name different parameter list, resolved by the compiler using the <em>reference/declared</em> type. Return type alone is not enough.</li>
<li>Overriding = runtime (dynamic) polymorphism, same signature in a subclass, resolved via the vtable at runtime using the <em>actual object</em> type. Rules: same or covariant return type, cannot reduce visibility, cannot throw broader <em>checked</em> exceptions, <code>static</code>/<code>private</code>/<code>final</code> methods cannot be overridden (static gets <em>hidden</em>).</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-4"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q4</span><span class="qtext">Why is <code>main</code> <code>public static void</code>?</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>public</code> so the JVM launcher can call it from outside the class; <code>static</code> so no instance is required before the entry point; <code>void</code> because nothing meaningful can be returned to the JVM (exit code comes from <code>System.exit</code>).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-5"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q5</span><span class="qtext"><code>equals()</code> and <code>hashCode()</code> contract.</span></p><div class="a"><ol>
<li>If <code>a.equals(b)</code> then <code>a.hashCode() == b.hashCode()</code>.</li>
<li>Equal hashCodes do <strong>not</strong> imply equality (collisions allowed).</li>
<li>Both must be consistent across calls unless the fields used change.</li>
<li><code>equals</code> must be reflexive, symmetric, transitive, consistent, and <code>x.equals(null)</code> is false.</li>
</ol><p>Break it and <code>HashMap</code>/<code>HashSet</code> misbehave: you insert an object, mutate a field used in <code>hashCode</code>, and can never retrieve it — a classic memory-leak/"lost entry" bug.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nd">@Override</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">boolean</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">equals</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Object</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">o</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">if</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="k">this</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">==</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">o</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kc">true</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">if</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="o">!</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">o</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">instanceof</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">e</span><span class="p">))</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kc">false</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// pattern matching, Java 16+</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Objects</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">equals</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">id</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">e</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">id</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
<span class="nd">@Override</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">hashCode</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Objects</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">hash</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">id</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-6"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q6</span><span class="qtext">Why are keys in HashMap usually immutable (String, Integer)?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Because hashCode must stay stable for the entry's lifetime in the map, and immutable types can cache the hash. Strings cache <code>hash</code> in a field.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-7"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q7</span><span class="qtext"><code>==</code> vs <code>equals</code> for Strings, and the String pool.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>==</code> compares references; <code>equals</code> compares content. String literals are interned in the string pool (in heap since Java 7); <code>new String("a")</code> creates a distinct heap object. <code>.intern()</code> pushes it into the pool.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">a</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"java"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">b</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"java"</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w">              </span><span class="c1">// a == b  → true (pool)</span>
<span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">c</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"java"</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">              </span><span class="c1">// a == c  → false</span>
<span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">d</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">c</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">intern</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w">                      </span><span class="c1">// a == d  → true</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-8"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q8</span><span class="qtext">Why is String immutable?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Security (class loading, file paths, DB URLs can't be mutated after validation), thread-safety without synchronisation, hash caching, and string-pool sharing.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-9"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q9</span><span class="qtext">String vs StringBuilder vs StringBuffer.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>String</code> immutable; <code>StringBuilder</code> mutable, not synchronised (use this); <code>StringBuffer</code> mutable, synchronised (legacy, slower). String concatenation in a loop is O(n²) — the compiler only optimises simple concatenations, not loops.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-10"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q10</span><span class="qtext"><code>final</code>, <code>finally</code>, <code>finalize</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>final</code> = non-reassignable variable / non-overridable method / non-extendable class. <code>finally</code> = block that always executes (except <code>System.exit</code>, JVM crash, infinite loop in try). <code>finalize()</code> = deprecated since Java 9, removed in 18 — use <code>try-with-resources</code>/<code>Cleaner</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-11"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q11</span><span class="qtext">Can <code>finally</code> be skipped? Does it override a return?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Skipped only on <code>System.exit()</code>, killing the process, or daemon-thread death. A <code>return</code> in <code>finally</code> swallows exceptions and overrides the try's return — never do it.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-12"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q12</span><span class="qtext">Shallow vs deep copy.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Shallow copies the reference fields (both objects share the inner objects); deep clones nested objects too. Implement deep copy via copy constructor, serialization round-trip, or a mapping library. <code>Object.clone()</code> is shallow and generally discouraged.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-13"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q13</span><span class="qtext">Checked vs unchecked exceptions — and what do you use in production?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Checked (<code>IOException</code>, <code>SQLException</code>) — compiler-enforced, recoverable. Unchecked (<code>RuntimeException</code> family) — programming errors. Errors (<code>OutOfMemoryError</code>, <code>StackOverflowError</code>) — don't catch.
In modern Spring services, most teams prefer <strong>unchecked</strong> custom exceptions (<code>ResourceNotFoundException extends RuntimeException</code>) plus a <code>@RestControllerAdvice</code> handler, because checked exceptions pollute signatures and don't compose with lambdas/streams.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-14"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q14</span><span class="qtext">try-with-resources and suppressed exceptions.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Any <code>AutoCloseable</code> is closed in reverse order automatically. If both the body and <code>close()</code> throw, the body's exception propagates and the close exception is attached via <code>getSuppressed()</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-15"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q15</span><span class="qtext">Custom exception example.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">InsufficientBalanceException</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">extends</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">RuntimeException</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">private</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">final</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">accountId</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">InsufficientBalanceException</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">accountId</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BigDecimal</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">shortfall</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="kd">super</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"Account %s short by %s"</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">formatted</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">accountId</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">shortfall</span><span class="p">));</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="k">this</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">accountId</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">accountId</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-16"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q16</span><span class="qtext">Why is <code>BigDecimal</code> used for money and never <code>double</code>?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Binary floating point can't represent 0.1 exactly → <code>0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3</code>. <code>BigDecimal</code> is arbitrary-precision decimal. Always construct from <code>String</code> or <code>BigDecimal.valueOf(double)</code>, never <code>new BigDecimal(0.1)</code>. Always specify scale + <code>RoundingMode.HALF_UP</code> (or HALF_EVEN for finance). Use <code>compareTo</code> not <code>equals</code> (<code>2.0</code> vs <code>2.00</code> are unequal by <code>equals</code>).
<em>Entain angle:</em> betting stakes, odds and settlement all need <code>BigDecimal</code> or long-minor-units (store pennies as <code>long</code>).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-17"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q17</span><span class="qtext">Pass by value or pass by reference?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Java is always <strong>pass by value</strong>. For objects, the <em>value of the reference</em> is copied — so you can mutate the pointed-to object, but reassigning the parameter doesn't affect the caller.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-18"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q18</span><span class="qtext"><code>static</code> block vs instance block vs constructor — execution order.</span></p><div class="a"><p>static blocks (once, at class load, parent first) → instance blocks → constructor. Parent's entire chain before child's.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-19"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q19</span><span class="qtext">What is an immutable class and how do you write one?</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">final</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">Money</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w">                        </span><span class="c1">// 1. final class</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">private</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">final</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BigDecimal</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">amount</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w">              </span><span class="c1">// 2. final private fields</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">private</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">final</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">tags</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">Money</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">BigDecimal</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">amount</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">tags</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="k">this</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">amount</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">amount</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="k">this</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">tags</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">List</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">copyOf</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">tags</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">            </span><span class="c1">// 3. defensive copy in</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">getTags</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">tags</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="c1">// 4. unmodifiable out</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="c1">// 5. no setters</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-20"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q20</span><span class="qtext">What is a <code>record</code> (Java 16)?</span></p><div class="a"><p>An immutable, transparent data carrier: auto-generates constructor, accessors, <code>equals</code>, <code>hashCode</code>, <code>toString</code>. Can have compact constructors for validation, static factories, and implement interfaces — but cannot extend a class.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">record</span> <span class="nc">BetRequest</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">marketId</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BigDecimal</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">stake</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BetRequest</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="k">if</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">stake</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">signum</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&lt;=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">throw</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">IllegalArgumentException</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"stake must be &gt; 0"</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Great for DTOs, value objects, and as <code>Map</code> keys.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-21"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q21</span><span class="qtext">Marker interface? Functional interface?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Marker = no methods, conveys metadata (<code>Serializable</code>, <code>Cloneable</code>) — mostly replaced by annotations. Functional = exactly one abstract method, usable as a lambda target; annotate <code>@FunctionalInterface</code> to enforce it.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-22"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q22</span><span class="qtext">Generics: what is type erasure? What is PECS?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Generics are compile-time only; the compiler erases type parameters to their bound (<code>Object</code> by default) and inserts casts. Consequences: no <code>new T[]</code>, no <code>instanceof List&lt;String&gt;</code>, can't overload on <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> vs <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code>.
<strong>PECS = Producer Extends, Consumer Super.</strong></p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kt">void</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">copy</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;?</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">extends</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Number</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">src</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// producer — we read from it</span>
<span class="w">          </span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;?</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">super</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Number</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">dst</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// consumer — we write into it</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">for</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Number</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">n</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">src</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">dst</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">add</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">n</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p><code>List&lt;?&gt;</code> = unknown type, read-only (except <code>null</code>).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-23"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q23</span><span class="qtext">Why can't you do <code>List&lt;String&gt; ls = new ArrayList&lt;Object&gt;()</code> or vice-versa?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Generics are invariant — needed for type safety. Arrays are covariant, which is why <code>Object[] o = new String[1]; o[0] = 1;</code> compiles but throws <code>ArrayStoreException</code> at runtime.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p1-2-collections-framework">2. Collections Framework<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p1-2-collections-framework">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q1-24"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q24</span><span class="qtext">Explain the hierarchy.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>Collection</code> → <code>List</code> (ArrayList, LinkedList, Vector, CopyOnWriteArrayList), <code>Set</code> (HashSet, LinkedHashSet, TreeSet), <code>Queue</code>/<code>Deque</code> (ArrayDeque, PriorityQueue, LinkedList, BlockingQueues). <code>Map</code> is separate: HashMap, LinkedHashMap, TreeMap, Hashtable, ConcurrentHashMap, EnumMap, WeakHashMap.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-25"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q25</span><span class="qtext">ArrayList vs LinkedList.</span></p><div class="a"><p>ArrayList: dynamic array, O(1) random access, O(n) mid-insert, cache-friendly, grows by ~1.5×. LinkedList: doubly linked, O(1) add/remove <em>given a node</em>, but O(n) to find it, high memory overhead. In practice <strong>ArrayList wins almost always</strong>; use <code>ArrayDeque</code> if you need a queue/stack.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-26"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q26</span><span class="qtext">How does HashMap work internally (Java 8+)?</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li>Array of <code>Node&lt;K,V&gt;[] table</code>, default capacity 16, load factor 0.75.</li>
<li><code>index = (n - 1) &amp; hash</code>, where <code>hash = h ^ (h &gt;&gt;&gt; 16)</code> (spreads high bits into low bits since the table is a power of two).</li>
<li>Collisions → singly linked list in the bucket; <strong>if a bucket exceeds 8 nodes and table size ≥ 64, it converts to a red-black tree</strong> (O(log n) instead of O(n)); untreeifies below 6.</li>
<li>Resize doubles capacity and rehashes; Java 8 splits each bucket into lo/hi lists preserving order (Java 7's reversal could cause an infinite loop under concurrency).</li>
<li>Allows one null key (bucket 0) and multiple null values.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-27"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q27</span><span class="qtext">HashMap vs Hashtable vs ConcurrentHashMap.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Hashtable: legacy, fully synchronised on the whole map, no nulls. ConcurrentHashMap: Java 8 uses CAS on the first node + <code>synchronized</code> on the bin head — lock granularity is per-bucket, allows full-concurrency reads, no nulls, <code>size()</code> is an estimate, iterators are weakly consistent (no <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-28"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q28</span><span class="qtext">Fail-fast vs fail-safe iterators.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Fail-fast (ArrayList, HashMap) track <code>modCount</code> and throw <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> if structurally modified during iteration. Fail-safe (<code>CopyOnWriteArrayList</code>, <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code>) iterate over a snapshot or tolerate changes. Fix for CME in single thread: use <code>Iterator.remove()</code> or <code>removeIf()</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-29"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q29</span><span class="qtext">HashSet vs TreeSet vs LinkedHashSet.</span></p><div class="a"><p>HashSet — O(1), unordered, backed by HashMap. LinkedHashSet — insertion order, doubly-linked. TreeSet — sorted, O(log n), red-black tree, requires <code>Comparable</code>/<code>Comparator</code>, no nulls.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-30"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q30</span><span class="qtext">Comparable vs Comparator.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>Comparable.compareTo</code> = natural ordering, inside the class, single. <code>Comparator.compare</code> = external, many strategies.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">list</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">sort</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Comparator</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">comparing</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getDept</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">                    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">thenComparing</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getSalary</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Comparator</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">reverseOrder</span><span class="p">())</span>
<span class="w">                    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">thenComparing</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getName</span><span class="p">));</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-31"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q31</span><span class="qtext">How to make a LRU cache in Java?</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">LinkedHashMap</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">K</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="n">V</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">16</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mf">0.75f</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kc">true</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// accessOrder = true</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">protected</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">boolean</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">removeEldestEntry</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Map</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">Entry</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">K</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="n">V</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">e</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">size</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">CAPACITY</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="p">};</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Wrap in <code>Collections.synchronizedMap</code> or use Caffeine/Guava in production.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-32"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q32</span><span class="qtext"><code>Collections.unmodifiableList</code> vs <code>List.of</code> vs <code>List.copyOf</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>unmodifiableList</code> is a <em>view</em> — mutating the backing list is visible. <code>List.of</code> (Java 9) is truly immutable, rejects nulls. <code>List.copyOf</code> makes an immutable snapshot.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-33"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q33</span><span class="qtext">Complexity cheat sheet.</span></p><div class="a"><table>
