<h2 id="p1-3-java-8-21-features-very-likely-at-5-yrs">3. Java 8 → 21 Features (very likely at 5+ yrs)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p1-3-java-8-21-features-very-likely-at-5-yrs">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q1-34"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q34</span><span class="qtext">Key Java 8 features.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Lambdas, functional interfaces, Stream API, <code>Optional</code>, default/static interface methods, method references, new Date/Time API (<code>java.time</code>), <code>CompletableFuture</code>, <code>Nashorn</code>, repeatable annotations, <code>StringJoiner</code>, PermGen → Metaspace.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-35"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q35</span><span class="qtext">Built-in functional interfaces.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>Function&lt;T,R&gt;</code>, <code>BiFunction</code>, <code>Supplier&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Consumer&lt;T&gt;</code>/<code>BiConsumer</code>, <code>Predicate&lt;T&gt;</code>/<code>BiPredicate</code>, <code>UnaryOperator&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>BinaryOperator&lt;T&gt;</code>, plus primitive specialisations (<code>IntPredicate</code>, <code>ToIntFunction</code>) that avoid boxing.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-36"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q36</span><span class="qtext">Intermediate vs terminal stream operations. What is laziness?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Intermediate (<code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>sorted</code>, <code>distinct</code>, <code>limit</code>, <code>peek</code>, <code>flatMap</code>) return a Stream and are lazy — nothing runs until a terminal op (<code>collect</code>, <code>forEach</code>, <code>reduce</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>anyMatch</code>, <code>findFirst</code>). Elements are pushed through the whole pipeline one at a time (except stateful ops like <code>sorted</code>), which enables short-circuiting.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-37"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q37</span><span class="qtext">map vs flatMap.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>map</code> is 1→1; <code>flatMap</code> is 1→many, flattening nested structures.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">allSkills</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">employees</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">()</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">flatMap</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">e</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">e</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">getSkills</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">())</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">distinct</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">sorted</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">toList</span><span class="p">();</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-38"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q38</span><span class="qtext">Common Collectors you must know.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">Map</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">byDept</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">emps</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">groupingBy</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getDept</span><span class="p">));</span>

<span class="n">Map</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Long</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">countByDept</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">emps</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">groupingBy</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getDept</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">counting</span><span class="p">()));</span>

<span class="n">Map</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Double</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">avgSalary</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">emps</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">groupingBy</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getDept</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">averagingDouble</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getSalary</span><span class="p">)));</span>

<span class="n">Map</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Boolean</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">split</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">emps</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">partitioningBy</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">e</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">e</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">getSalary</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">100000</span><span class="p">));</span>

<span class="n">Map</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Optional</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">topPaidPerDept</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">emps</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">groupingBy</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getDept</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">maxBy</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">comparing</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getSalary</span><span class="p">))));</span>

<span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">names</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">emps</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">map</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getName</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">joining</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">", "</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"["</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"]"</span><span class="p">));</span>

<span class="n">Map</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Long</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">idToName</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">emps</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">toMap</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getId</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getName</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">a</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="n">b</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">a</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">LinkedHashMap</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="k">new</span><span class="p">));</span>
</code></pre></div><p>⚠️ <code>toMap</code> throws <code>IllegalStateException</code> on duplicate keys unless you supply a merge function; it also NPEs on null values (unlike <code>groupingBy</code>).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-39"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q39</span><span class="qtext">Frequently asked stream one-liners.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="c1">// 2nd highest salary</span>
<span class="n">emps</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">map</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getSalary</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">distinct</span><span class="p">()</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">sorted</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">reverseOrder</span><span class="p">()).</span><span class="na">skip</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">findFirst</span><span class="p">();</span>

<span class="c1">// first non-repeating character</span>
<span class="n">str</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">chars</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">mapToObj</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">c</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="kt">char</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">c</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">   </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">groupingBy</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">identity</span><span class="p">(),</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">LinkedHashMap</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="k">new</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">counting</span><span class="p">()))</span>
<span class="w">   </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">entrySet</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">filter</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">e</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">e</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">getValue</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">==</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">   </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">map</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Map</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">Entry</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getKey</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">findFirst</span><span class="p">();</span>

<span class="c1">// frequency of words</span>
<span class="n">Arrays</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">text</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">split</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"\\s+"</span><span class="p">)).</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">groupingBy</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">identity</span><span class="p">(),</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">counting</span><span class="p">()));</span>

<span class="c1">// find duplicates in a list</span>
<span class="n">Set</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">seen</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">HashSet</span><span class="o">&lt;&gt;</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="n">list</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">filter</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">n</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">!</span><span class="n">seen</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">add</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">n</span><span class="p">)).</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">toSet</span><span class="p">());</span>

<span class="c1">// sum with reduce</span>
<span class="n">list</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">reduce</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Integer</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">sum</span><span class="p">);</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-40"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q40</span><span class="qtext">When is a parallel stream a bad idea?</span></p><div class="a"><p>When the source is hard to split (LinkedList, IO streams), the work per element is tiny, the operation is ordered/stateful, you're inside a servlet container already saturating cores, or you mutate shared state. All parallel streams share the <strong>common ForkJoinPool</strong> (<code>n-1</code> threads) — a slow blocking task there starves the whole JVM. Never do blocking IO in a parallel stream.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-41"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q41</span><span class="qtext"><code>Optional</code> — correct use.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Return type for "may be absent"; <strong>not</strong> for fields, parameters, or collections (return an empty collection instead). Never call <code>get()</code> without <code>isPresent()</code>; prefer <code>orElse</code>, <code>orElseGet</code> (lazy), <code>orElseThrow</code>, <code>map</code>, <code>flatMap</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>ifPresentOrElse</code>.
<code>orElse</code> always evaluates its argument; <code>orElseGet</code> only on absence — matters when the fallback is expensive.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-42"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q42</span><span class="qtext">Java 9–21 features worth naming.</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li><strong>9</strong>: modules (JPMS), <code>List.of</code>, <code>Stream.takeWhile/dropWhile/iterate</code>, private interface methods, JShell, <code>Optional.stream()</code>.</li>
<li><strong>10</strong>: <code>var</code> local type inference, <code>Collectors.toUnmodifiableList</code>.</li>
<li><strong>11 (LTS)</strong>: HTTP Client, <code>String.isBlank/lines/strip/repeat</code>, <code>Files.readString</code>, <code>var</code> in lambda params, single-file source launch.</li>
<li><strong>14/16</strong>: <code>switch</code> expressions with <code>yield</code>, <code>instanceof</code> pattern matching, <strong>records</strong>, helpful NPEs.</li>
<li><strong>15/17 (LTS)</strong>: text blocks <code>"""</code>, <strong>sealed classes</strong>, pattern matching for switch (preview), removal of RMI activation.</li>
<li><strong>21 (LTS)</strong>: <strong>virtual threads (Project Loom)</strong>, sequenced collections (<code>getFirst/getLast</code>), pattern matching for switch + record patterns (final), structured concurrency (preview), generational ZGC.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-43"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q43</span><span class="qtext">What are virtual threads and why do they matter?</span></p><div class="a"><p>JDK 21 lightweight threads scheduled by the JVM on a small pool of carrier (platform) threads. When a virtual thread blocks on IO it <em>unmounts</em> from its carrier, so you can have millions of them. This makes the simple thread-per-request blocking style scale like reactive code, without the reactive programming model.
Caveats: pinning when blocking inside <code>synchronized</code> blocks or native frames (use <code>ReentrantLock</code> instead), thread-locals become expensive at scale, don't pool them (<code>Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()</code>).</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="k">try</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="kd">var</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">executor</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Executors</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor</span><span class="p">())</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">IntStream</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">range</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">10_000</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">forEach</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">i</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">executor</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">submit</span><span class="p">(()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">callApi</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">i</span><span class="p">)));</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-44"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q44</span><span class="qtext">Sealed classes.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">sealed</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">interface</span> <span class="nc">BetOutcome</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">permits</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Won</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Lost</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Void_</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{}</span>
<span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">record</span> <span class="nc">Won</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">BigDecimal</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">payout</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">implements</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BetOutcome</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Restricts the hierarchy so <code>switch</code> can be <strong>exhaustive</strong> without a <code>default</code> — makes ADT-style modelling safe.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p1-4-jvm-memory-gc">4. JVM, Memory &amp; GC<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p1-4-jvm-memory-gc">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q1-45"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q45</span><span class="qtext">JVM memory areas.</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li><strong>Heap</strong> — objects; split into Young (Eden + 2 Survivors) and Old/Tenured.</li>
<li><strong>Metaspace</strong> (Java 8+, native memory, replaced PermGen) — class metadata.</li>
<li><strong>Stack</strong> — per thread, frames with locals &amp; partial results; <code>StackOverflowError</code>.</li>
<li><strong>PC register</strong>, <strong>Native method stack</strong>.</li>
<li>Code cache (JIT-compiled code), direct/off-heap buffers.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-46"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q46</span><span class="qtext">How does GC work? Explain generational collection.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Weak generational hypothesis: most objects die young. Minor GC cleans Eden + survivors (copying collector — fast, stop-the-world but short); survivors age, and after a threshold get promoted to Old. Major/Full GC cleans the Old gen (mark-sweep-compact — expensive).
Reachability is determined from <strong>GC roots</strong> (stack locals, static fields, JNI refs, active threads), not reference counting — so cycles are collected fine.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-47"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q47</span><span class="qtext">Compare GC algorithms.</span></p><div class="a"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>GC</th>
<th>Best for</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Serial</td>
<td>tiny heaps/containers</td>
<td>single-threaded</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Parallel (Throughput)</td>
<td>batch jobs</td>
<td>max throughput, longer pauses</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>G1</strong> (default 9+)</td>
<td>balanced, heaps 4–32GB+</td>
<td>region-based, <code>-XX:MaxGCPauseMillis</code> target</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ZGC</td>
<td>low latency, huge heaps</td>
<td>sub-millisecond pauses, concurrent, colored pointers</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Shenandoah</td>
<td>low latency</td>
<td>concurrent compaction</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table><p><em>Entain / trading-style answer:</em> for a low-latency bet-placement service you'd tune for pause time (G1 with a pause target or ZGC), keep allocation rate low, avoid huge object churn, and size the young gen generously so most garbage dies in Eden.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-48"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q48</span><span class="qtext">Can you force GC? Does <code>System.gc()</code> work?</span></p><div class="a"><p>It's a <em>hint</em>. Can be disabled with <code>-XX:+DisableExplicitGC</code>. Never rely on it.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-49"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q49</span><span class="qtext">Types of references.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Strong (never collected while reachable) → SoftReference (cleared before OOM — caches) → WeakReference (cleared at next GC — <code>WeakHashMap</code>, canonicalising maps) → PhantomReference (post-mortem cleanup, with a <code>ReferenceQueue</code>).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-50"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q50</span><span class="qtext">What causes memory leaks in Java, if there's a GC?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Unintentional strong references: static collections that only grow, unclosed resources, listeners/callbacks never deregistered, <code>ThreadLocal</code> not removed in a pooled thread, ClassLoader leaks on hot redeploy, mutating a key after it's in a HashMap, unbounded caches, <code>String.substring</code> in old JVMs.
<strong>How to diagnose:</strong> monitor heap after full GC trending upward → take a heap dump (<code>jmap -dump:live,format=b,file=heap.hprof &lt;pid&gt;</code> or <code>-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError</code>) → analyse in <strong>Eclipse MAT</strong> with the Leak Suspects report / dominator tree → find the GC root path. Live tools: <code>jcmd</code>, <code>jstat -gcutil</code>, JFR (<code>-XX:StartFlightRecording</code>), VisualVM, async-profiler.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-51"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q51</span><span class="qtext"><code>OutOfMemoryError</code> variants.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>Java heap space</code>, <code>GC overhead limit exceeded</code> (98% time in GC, &lt;2% recovered), <code>Metaspace</code>, <code>Unable to create new native thread</code>, <code>Direct buffer memory</code>, <code>Requested array size exceeds VM limit</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-52"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q52</span><span class="qtext">Key JVM flags you've used.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>-Xms/-Xmx</code> (set equal in containers to avoid resize), <code>-XX:MaxMetaspaceSize</code>, <code>-XX:+UseG1GC</code>, <code>-XX:MaxGCPauseMillis=200</code>, <code>-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError -XX:HeapDumpPath=</code>, <code>-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75</code> (container-aware, better than -Xmx in K8s), <code>-XX:+UseContainerSupport</code> (on by default), <code>-Xss</code> for stack size.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-53"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q53</span><span class="qtext">What is JIT? What are C1/C2 and tiered compilation?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Bytecode is interpreted first; hot methods (invocation/loop counters) are compiled to native code. C1 compiles fast with light optimisation, C2 compiles slowly with aggressive optimisation (inlining, escape analysis, loop unrolling, dead-code elimination). Tiered compilation uses C1 then promotes to C2. Deoptimisation happens when a speculative assumption breaks.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-54"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q54</span><span class="qtext">What is escape analysis?</span></p><div class="a"><p>If the JIT proves an object never escapes a method, it can allocate it on the stack (scalar replacement) and elide locks (lock coarsening/elision).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-55"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q55</span><span class="qtext">Class loading.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Loading → Linking (verify, prepare, resolve) → Initialisation (static blocks). Loaders: Bootstrap → Platform/Extension → Application, with the <strong>parent delegation</strong> model (child asks parent first) preventing spoofing of <code>java.lang.String</code>.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p1-5-concurrency-multithreading-heavily-asked">5. Concurrency &amp; Multithreading (heavily asked)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p1-5-concurrency-multithreading-heavily-asked">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q1-56"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q56</span><span class="qtext">Thread lifecycle.</span></p><div class="a"><p>NEW → RUNNABLE → (BLOCKED / WAITING / TIMED_WAITING) → TERMINATED.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-57"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q57</span><span class="qtext"><code>Runnable</code> vs <code>Callable</code> vs <code>Future</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>Runnable.run()</code> returns void, can't throw checked exceptions. <code>Callable.call()</code> returns <code>V</code> and can throw. <code>Future</code> is the handle: <code>get()</code> (blocking), <code>isDone()</code>, <code>cancel(mayInterrupt)</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-58"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q58</span><span class="qtext">What is the Java Memory Model? Explain happens-before.</span></p><div class="a"><p>The JMM defines when a write by one thread becomes visible to another. Without synchronisation, the compiler/CPU may reorder and cache values in registers. <strong>happens-before</strong> edges are created by: program order in a thread, <code>unlock</code> → subsequent <code>lock</code> of the same monitor, <code>volatile</code> write → subsequent read, <code>Thread.start()</code> → everything in that thread, everything in a thread → <code>join()</code> returning, and constructor completion → final field reads.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-59"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q59</span><span class="qtext"><code>volatile</code> — what does it guarantee, what doesn't it?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Guarantees <strong>visibility</strong> (reads/writes go to main memory) and prevents reordering across the access (memory barrier). Does <strong>not</strong> guarantee atomicity of compound operations — <code>count++</code> is read-modify-write and is still racy.
Use for: flags (<code>volatile boolean running</code>), the double-checked locking singleton, safe publication of an immutable object.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-60"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q60</span><span class="qtext"><code>synchronized</code> — what does it lock?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Instance method → <code>this</code>; static method → <code>Class</code> object; block → the given object. Reentrant. Provides mutual exclusion <strong>and</strong> visibility (acquire/release semantics). Prefer locking a private final object over <code>this</code> to avoid outside interference.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-61"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q61</span><span class="qtext"><code>synchronized</code> vs <code>ReentrantLock</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>synchronized</th>
<th>ReentrantLock</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Acquisition</td>
<td>implicit, block-structured</td>
<td>explicit <code>lock()/unlock()</code> in finally</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Try / timeout</td>
<td>no</td>
<td><code>tryLock(timeout)</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Interruptible</td>
<td>no</td>
<td><code>lockInterruptibly()</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Fairness</td>
<td>no</td>
<td>optional fair mode</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Conditions</td>
<td>one wait set</td>
<td>multiple <code>Condition</code>s</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Loom</td>
<td>pins virtual thread</td>
<td>does not pin</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-62"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q62</span><span class="qtext"><code>wait/notify</code> vs <code>Condition await/signal</code>. Why must <code>wait</code> be in a loop?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Both release the monitor while waiting. Always <code>while (!condition) wait();</code> — because of spurious wakeups and because another thread may consume the condition before you're rescheduled.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-63"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q63</span><span class="qtext">Producer–consumer implementation.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="c1">// Modern way — no manual locking</span>
<span class="n">BlockingQueue</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Order</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">queue</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">LinkedBlockingQueue</span><span class="o">&lt;&gt;</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">1000</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="c1">// producer</span>
<span class="n">queue</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">put</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">order</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">          </span><span class="c1">// blocks when full → natural back-pressure</span>
<span class="c1">// consumer</span>
<span class="n">Order</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">o</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">queue</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">take</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w">    </span><span class="c1">// blocks when empty</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Classic version with <code>wait/notifyAll</code> on a shared buffer is the follow-up they may ask you to write.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-64"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q64</span><span class="qtext">Deadlock — cause, detection, prevention.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Coffman conditions: mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait. Prevent by imposing a <strong>global lock ordering</strong>, using <code>tryLock</code> with timeout, reducing lock scope, or using lock-free structures. Detect with a thread dump (<code>jstack</code>, <code>jcmd Thread.print</code>) — the JVM prints "Found one Java-level deadlock". Related: <strong>livelock</strong> (threads keep responding to each other, no progress), <strong>starvation</strong> (a thread never gets the lock).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-65"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q65</span><span class="qtext">Explain the Executor framework and thread pool sizing.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">ExecutorService</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">pool</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ThreadPoolExecutor</span><span class="p">(</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">corePoolSize</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">maxPoolSize</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">keepAlive</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">TimeUnit</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">SECONDS</span><span class="p">,</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ArrayBlockingQueue</span><span class="o">&lt;&gt;</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">500</span><span class="p">),</span><span class="w">          </span><span class="c1">// bounded! </span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ThreadFactoryBuilder</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">setNameFormat</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"bet-%d"</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">build</span><span class="p">(),</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ThreadPoolExecutor</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">CallerRunsPolicy</span><span class="p">());</span><span class="w">  </span><span class="c1">// back-pressure</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Key behaviour: threads grow to core → then the <strong>queue fills</strong> → only then grow to max → then the rejection policy fires. So an unbounded <code>LinkedBlockingQueue</code> (what <code>newFixedThreadPool</code> uses) means max is never reached and you OOM instead. That's why hand-rolling <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code> beats <code>Executors.*</code> factories.
Sizing: CPU-bound ≈ <code>nCores + 1</code>; IO-bound ≈ <code>nCores × (1 + waitTime/serviceTime)</code>. Then measure.
Rejection policies: <code>AbortPolicy</code> (default, throws), <code>CallerRunsPolicy</code>, <code>DiscardPolicy</code>, <code>DiscardOldestPolicy</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-66"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q66</span><span class="qtext"><code>submit</code> vs <code>execute</code>. Where do exceptions go?</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>execute(Runnable)</code> — exception propagates to the thread's <code>UncaughtExceptionHandler</code>. <code>submit()</code> — exception is <strong>captured in the Future</strong> and only surfaces as <code>ExecutionException</code> when you call <code>get()</code>. Silent failures come from ignoring futures.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-67"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q67</span><span class="qtext"><code>shutdown</code> vs <code>shutdownNow</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>shutdown()</code> — no new tasks, finish queued ones. <code>shutdownNow()</code> — interrupts running tasks and returns the pending list. Standard pattern: <code>shutdown()</code> → <code>awaitTermination(30s)</code> → <code>shutdownNow()</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-68"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q68</span><span class="qtext">Atomic classes &amp; CAS.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>AtomicInteger/Long/Reference</code> use <code>compareAndSet</code> on top of the CPU's CAS instruction — lock-free, optimistic, spin on failure. Problems: ABA (solved by <code>AtomicStampedReference</code>), and high contention causes wasted spins — <code>LongAdder</code> fixes this by striping counters across cells and summing on read. Use <code>LongAdder</code> for hot metrics counters.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-69"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q69</span><span class="qtext">ConcurrentHashMap atomic methods.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>putIfAbsent</code>, <code>computeIfAbsent</code>, <code>compute</code>, <code>merge</code>. Don't do <code>if (!map.containsKey(k)) map.put(k, v)</code> — that's a race.
⚠️ Never perform a long/blocking operation or modify the same map inside <code>computeIfAbsent</code> — the bin is locked.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-70"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q70</span><span class="qtext"><code>CountDownLatch</code> vs <code>CyclicBarrier</code> vs <code>Semaphore</code> vs <code>Phaser</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Latch — one-shot, N threads wait for N countdowns (wait for services to start). Barrier — reusable, N threads wait for each other, optional barrier action. Semaphore — permits, rate/resource limiting. Phaser — flexible multi-phase barrier.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-71"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q71</span><span class="qtext"><code>CompletableFuture</code> — why and how?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Non-blocking composition, unlike <code>Future.get()</code>.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">CompletableFuture</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Odds</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">odds</span><span class="w">  </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">CompletableFuture</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">supplyAsync</span><span class="p">(()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">oddsSvc</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">get</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">id</span><span class="p">),</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">pool</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="n">CompletableFuture</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Limit</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">limit</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">CompletableFuture</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">supplyAsync</span><span class="p">(()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">limitSvc</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">get</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">user</span><span class="p">),</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">pool</span><span class="p">);</span>

<span class="n">odds</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">thenCombine</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">limit</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BetValidator</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">validate</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">thenApply</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="k">this</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">persist</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">orTimeout</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">500</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">MILLISECONDS</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">exceptionally</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">ex</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">log</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">error</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"bet failed"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ex</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Bet</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">rejected</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">})</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">thenAccept</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">publisher</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">publish</span><span class="p">);</span>
</code></pre></div><p><code>thenApply</code> (sync, same thread) vs <code>thenApplyAsync</code> (another pool). <code>thenCompose</code> = flatMap (avoids nested futures), <code>thenCombine</code> = zip two, <code>allOf</code>/<code>anyOf</code> for fan-out. <strong>Always pass your own Executor</strong> — the default is the common ForkJoinPool.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-72"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q72</span><span class="qtext"><code>ThreadLocal</code> — use and danger.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Per-thread storage (<code>SecurityContextHolder</code>, MDC for correlation IDs, <code>SimpleDateFormat</code> caching). Danger: in a thread pool the thread is reused, so stale data leaks into the next request and the value is never GC'd → <strong>always <code>remove()</code> in a finally block</strong>. Use <code>InheritableThreadLocal</code> carefully; with virtual threads prefer <code>ScopedValue</code> (21 preview).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-73"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q73</span><span class="qtext">What is ForkJoinPool / work stealing?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Divide-and-conquer via <code>RecursiveTask</code>/<code>RecursiveAction</code>; each worker has a deque and idle workers steal from the tail of others' deques. Backs parallel streams.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-74"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q74</span><span class="qtext">How would you make a class thread-safe?</span></p><div class="a"><p>In order of preference: (1) make it immutable, (2) confine state to one thread, (3) use concurrent collections/atomics, (4) synchronise with a documented locking policy, (5) use <code>@GuardedBy</code> annotations to document it.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-75"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q75</span><span class="qtext">Double-checked locking singleton — and why the enum is better.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">private</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">static</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">volatile</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Singleton</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">instance</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// volatile is mandatory</span>
<span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">static</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Singleton</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">getInstance</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">if</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">instance</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">==</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kc">null</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="kd">synchronized</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Singleton</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">class</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">            </span><span class="k">if</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">instance</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">==</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kc">null</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">instance</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Singleton</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">instance</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Without <code>volatile</code> another thread can see a <strong>partially constructed</strong> object due to reordering of allocate/construct/assign. <code>enum Singleton { INSTANCE; }</code> is serialization- and reflection-safe and simpler. In Spring you just use a singleton bean.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q1-76"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q76</span><span class="qtext">How do you handle an <code>InterruptedException</code>?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Either propagate it, or restore the flag: <code>Thread.currentThread().interrupt();</code>. Never swallow it silently — you destroy the cancellation signal.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p1-6-rapid-fire-coding-problems-be-ready-to-write-these-live">6. Rapid-fire coding problems (be ready to write these live)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p1-6-rapid-fire-coding-problems-be-ready-to-write-these-live">#</a></h2>
<ol>
<li>Reverse a string / check palindrome (with and without library).</li>
<li>Find duplicates / first non-repeating char in a string.</li>
<li>Two Sum (HashMap, O(n)); Three Sum (sort + two pointers).</li>
<li>Longest substring without repeating characters (sliding window).</li>
<li>Merge two sorted arrays / merge intervals.</li>
<li>Move zeros to end in place; Dutch national flag (sort 0/1/2).</li>
<li>Kadane's algorithm — max subarray sum.</li>
<li>Reverse a linked list (iterative + recursive); detect cycle (Floyd).</li>
<li>Find Nth from end of a linked list; find middle.</li>
<li>Balanced parentheses (Stack); min-stack in O(1).</li>
<li>Binary search + first/last occurrence; search in rotated sorted array.</li>
<li>BFS/DFS of a tree; level-order printing; height; LCA; validate BST.</li>
<li>Fibonacci with memoisation; climbing stairs (DP intro).</li>
<li>Anagram grouping (sorted-key HashMap).</li>
<li>Top K frequent elements (HashMap + PriorityQueue).</li>
<li>Implement LRU cache (HashMap + doubly linked list).</li>
<li>Producer–consumer with <code>BlockingQueue</code> and with <code>wait/notify</code>.</li>
<li>Print odd/even numbers alternately with two threads (classic Entain-style).</li>
<li>Stream-based: group employees by department, find the highest paid per dept, sort by multiple fields.</li>
<li>Custom <code>Comparator</code> sorting on an object list.</li>
</ol>
