<section class="panel" id="panel-2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-2" hidden><header class="part-head"><span class="part-n">Part 02</span><h1>Spring, JPA, SQL &amp; Microservices</h1><p class="kicker">The framework layer <span class="dot">·</span> 77 questions</p></header><div class="prose">
<blockquote>
<p>Applies to <strong>both EPAM and Entain</strong>. Entain will push harder on transactions, concurrency, idempotency and Kafka; EPAM will push on breadth + clean code.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p2-1-spring-core">1. Spring Core<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p2-1-spring-core">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q2-1"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q1</span><span class="qtext">What is IoC and Dependency Injection?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Inversion of Control = the framework, not your code, creates and wires objects. DI is the mechanism. Benefits: loose coupling, testability (inject mocks), configuration externalised, lifecycle management, AOP hooks.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-2"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q2</span><span class="qtext">Constructor vs setter vs field injection — which and why?</span></p><div class="a"><p><strong>Constructor injection</strong> is correct: dependencies are final and mandatory, the object is never in a half-built state, it's trivially testable without Spring, and circular dependencies fail fast at startup. Field injection (<code>@Autowired</code> on a field) hides dependencies, needs reflection to test, and allows unlimited dependencies (hiding SRP violations). Setter injection is for genuinely optional dependencies.
With one constructor, <code>@Autowired</code> is optional (Spring 4.3+). With Lombok, <code>@RequiredArgsConstructor</code> + <code>private final</code> fields.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-3"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q3</span><span class="qtext">How does Spring resolve ambiguity when there are two beans of the same type?</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>@Primary</code>, <code>@Qualifier("name")</code>, matching the field/parameter name to the bean name, or <code>@Profile</code>. Also <code>List&lt;PaymentProcessor&gt;</code> injects all implementations (great for the Strategy pattern), and <code>Map&lt;String, PaymentProcessor&gt;</code> gives you name→bean.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-4"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q4</span><span class="qtext">Bean scopes.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>singleton</code> (default, one per container — <em>not</em> per JVM), <code>prototype</code> (new instance per lookup; Spring does not manage its destruction), <code>request</code>, <code>session</code>, <code>application</code>, <code>websocket</code>.
Gotcha: injecting a prototype into a singleton gives you the same instance forever — fix with <code>@Lookup</code>, <code>ObjectProvider&lt;T&gt;</code>, or a scoped proxy (<code>@Scope(value="prototype", proxyMode=TARGET_CLASS)</code>).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-5"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q5</span><span class="qtext">Is a singleton bean thread-safe?</span></p><div class="a"><p>No. Spring only guarantees a single instance. If it holds mutable state you must synchronise it. Standard practice: keep beans <strong>stateless</strong>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-6"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q6</span><span class="qtext">Bean lifecycle.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Instantiate → populate properties → <code>BeanNameAware</code>/<code>BeanFactoryAware</code>/<code>ApplicationContextAware</code> → <code>BeanPostProcessor.postProcessBeforeInitialization</code> → <code>@PostConstruct</code> → <code>InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet</code> → custom <code>initMethod</code> → <code>postProcessAfterInitialization</code> (<strong>this is where AOP proxies are created</strong>) → bean in use → <code>@PreDestroy</code> → <code>DisposableBean.destroy</code> → <code>destroyMethod</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-7"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q7</span><span class="qtext"><code>BeanFactoryPostProcessor</code> vs <code>BeanPostProcessor</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p>The former modifies bean <em>definitions</em> before instantiation (e.g. <code>PropertySourcesPlaceholderConfigurer</code>); the latter modifies bean <em>instances</em> after creation (e.g. <code>@Transactional</code> proxying, <code>AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor</code>).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-8"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q8</span><span class="qtext"><code>@Component</code> vs <code>@Service</code> vs <code>@Repository</code> vs <code>@Controller</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p>All are <code>@Component</code> stereotypes; the distinction is semantic — except <code>@Repository</code>, which additionally enables <strong>exception translation</strong> (vendor <code>SQLException</code> → Spring's <code>DataAccessException</code> hierarchy) via a post-processor.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-9"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q9</span><span class="qtext"><code>@Configuration</code> vs <code>@Component</code> for <code>@Bean</code> methods.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>@Configuration</code> classes are CGLIB-proxied so that calling one <code>@Bean</code> method from another returns the <strong>same singleton</strong>. In a plain <code>@Component</code> (a.k.a. "lite mode") each call creates a new object.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-10"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q10</span><span class="qtext">How does <code>@Transactional</code> actually work?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Spring creates a proxy (JDK dynamic proxy if the bean implements an interface, otherwise CGLIB). The proxy opens a transaction via the <code>PlatformTransactionManager</code>, binds the <code>Connection</code>/<code>EntityManager</code> to a <code>ThreadLocal</code> (<code>TransactionSynchronizationManager</code>), then commits or rolls back.
<strong>Classic gotchas — expect this question:</strong></p><ul>
<li><strong>Self-invocation doesn't work.</strong> Calling <code>this.saveX()</code> from another method in the same class bypasses the proxy. Fix: move to another bean, self-inject, or use <code>AopContext.currentProxy()</code>.</li>
<li>Only <strong>public</strong> methods are proxied (with Spring AOP).</li>
<li>Default rollback only on <strong>unchecked</strong> exceptions. Checked exceptions commit unless you set <code>rollbackFor = Exception.class</code>.</li>
<li>Catching the exception inside the method means no rollback.</li>
<li><code>@Transactional</code> on a <code>private</code>/<code>final</code> method or a <code>final</code> class silently does nothing with CGLIB.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-11"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q11</span><span class="qtext">Propagation levels.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>REQUIRED</code> (default — join or create), <code>REQUIRES_NEW</code> (suspend outer, new tx — use for audit logs that must survive a rollback), <code>NESTED</code> (savepoint), <code>SUPPORTS</code>, <code>NOT_SUPPORTED</code>, <code>MANDATORY</code>, <code>NEVER</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-12"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q12</span><span class="qtext">Isolation levels and the anomalies they prevent.</span></p><div class="a"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Level</th>
<th>Dirty read</th>
<th>Non-repeatable read</th>
<th>Phantom</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>READ_UNCOMMITTED</td>
<td>✗</td>
<td>✗</td>
<td>✗</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>READ_COMMITTED (Postgres/Oracle default)</td>
<td>✓</td>
<td>✗</td>
<td>✗</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>REPEATABLE_READ (MySQL InnoDB default)</td>
<td>✓</td>
<td>✓</td>
<td>✗ (✓ in InnoDB via gap locks)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SERIALIZABLE</td>
<td>✓</td>
<td>✓</td>
<td>✓</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-13"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q13</span><span class="qtext">What is AOP? Explain the terms.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Cross-cutting concerns (logging, security, transactions, metrics) separated from business code. <strong>Aspect</strong> = the module; <strong>Join point</strong> = a point of execution (method call in Spring); <strong>Pointcut</strong> = expression selecting join points; <strong>Advice</strong> = <code>@Before</code>, <code>@After</code>, <code>@AfterReturning</code>, <code>@AfterThrowing</code>, <code>@Around</code>; <strong>Weaving</strong> = applying it (runtime proxying in Spring, compile-time in AspectJ).</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nd">@Aspect</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nd">@Component</span>
<span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">TimingAspect</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="nd">@Around</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"@annotation(com.x.Timed)"</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Object</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">time</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">ProceedingJoinPoint</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">pjp</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">throws</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Throwable</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="kt">long</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">t</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">System</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">nanoTime</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="k">try</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">pjp</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">proceed</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="k">finally</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">log</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">info</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"{} took {}ms"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">pjp</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">getSignature</span><span class="p">(),</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">System</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">nanoTime</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="o">-</span><span class="n">t</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="o">/</span><span class="mi">1_000_000</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p2-2-spring-boot">2. Spring Boot<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p2-2-spring-boot">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q2-14"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q14</span><span class="qtext">What problems does Spring Boot solve?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Auto-configuration, starter dependencies (curated, version-aligned), embedded server (no WAR deployment), externalised config with profiles, production-ready Actuator endpoints, opinionated defaults with easy overrides.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-15"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q15</span><span class="qtext">How does auto-configuration work internally?</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>@SpringBootApplication</code> = <code>@Configuration</code> + <code>@ComponentScan</code> + <code>@EnableAutoConfiguration</code>. The latter imports <code>AutoConfigurationImportSelector</code>, which reads candidate configuration classes from <code>META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports</code> (previously <code>spring.factories</code>) on the classpath. Each is guarded by <code>@Conditional</code> annotations: <code>@ConditionalOnClass</code>, <code>@ConditionalOnMissingBean</code>, <code>@ConditionalOnProperty</code>, <code>@ConditionalOnWebApplication</code>. <code>@ConditionalOnMissingBean</code> is why <em>your</em> bean always wins.
Debug with <code>--debug</code> to print the Condition Evaluation Report; exclude with <code>@SpringBootApplication(exclude = DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-16"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q16</span><span class="qtext">How do you write your own starter?</span></p><div class="a"><p>An <code>xxx-spring-boot-autoconfigure</code> module with <code>@AutoConfiguration</code> classes + conditionals + <code>@ConfigurationProperties</code>, registered in <code>AutoConfiguration.imports</code>, plus a thin <code>xxx-spring-boot-starter</code> pom that pulls in dependencies.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-17"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q17</span><span class="qtext">Property precedence / externalised config.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Command-line args &gt; <code>SPRING_APPLICATION_JSON</code> &gt; OS env vars &gt; system properties &gt; profile-specific <code>application-{profile}.yml</code> &gt; <code>application.yml</code> &gt; <code>@PropertySource</code> &gt; defaults. Config Server / Vault / K8s ConfigMaps plug in as additional property sources.
<code>@ConfigurationProperties(prefix="app.betting")</code> with validation (<code>@Validated</code>, JSR-380) is preferred over scattered <code>@Value</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-18"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q18</span><span class="qtext">Actuator — which endpoints do you use in production?</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>/health</code> (with liveness/readiness groups for K8s probes), <code>/info</code>, <code>/metrics</code> + <code>/prometheus</code> (Micrometer), <code>/loggers</code> (change log level at runtime — very useful in incidents), <code>/env</code>, <code>/threaddump</code>, <code>/heapdump</code>, <code>/httpexchanges</code>. Secure them: separate management port, exposed selectively, behind auth.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-19"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q19</span><span class="qtext">Profiles.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>@Profile("dev")</code>, <code>spring.profiles.active</code>, <code>application-prod.yml</code>. Use for datasource swaps, mock vs real integrations, and turning off schema auto-generation in prod.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p2-3-rest-api-design">3. REST API Design<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p2-3-rest-api-design">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q2-20"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q20</span><span class="qtext">Core REST principles.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Stateless, client–server, uniform interface, cacheable, layered, resource-oriented URIs (nouns, plural), HTTP verbs for semantics, HATEOAS (rarely implemented).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-21"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q21</span><span class="qtext">Idempotency of HTTP methods.</span></p><div class="a"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Method</th>
<th>Safe</th>
<th>Idempotent</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>GET, HEAD, OPTIONS</td>
<td>✓</td>
<td>✓</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>PUT, DELETE</td>
<td>✗</td>
<td>✓</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>POST, PATCH</td>
<td>✗</td>
<td>✗</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table><p><strong>How do you make POST idempotent?</strong> Client sends an <code>Idempotency-Key</code> header; server stores key → response in Redis/DB with a unique constraint; a repeat key returns the stored response instead of re-executing. <strong>This is a near-guaranteed Entain question</strong> (a retried bet-placement must not double-charge).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-22"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q22</span><span class="qtext">Status codes you'd use.</span></p><div class="a"><p>200 OK, 201 Created (+<code>Location</code>), 202 Accepted (async), 204 No Content, 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized (not authenticated), 403 Forbidden (authenticated, not allowed), 404, 409 Conflict (optimistic lock/duplicate), 412 Precondition Failed (ETag), 422 Unprocessable Entity (validation), 429 Too Many Requests (+<code>Retry-After</code>), 500, 503.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-23"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q23</span><span class="qtext">Global exception handling.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nd">@RestControllerAdvice</span>
<span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">ApiExceptionHandler</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="nd">@ExceptionHandler</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">ResourceNotFoundException</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">class</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">ResponseEntity</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">ProblemDetail</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">notFound</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">ResourceNotFoundException</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ex</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="kd">var</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">pd</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ProblemDetail</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">forStatusAndDetail</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">HttpStatus</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">NOT_FOUND</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ex</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">getMessage</span><span class="p">());</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">pd</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">setTitle</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"Resource not found"</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">pd</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">setProperty</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"traceId"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">MDC</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">get</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"traceId"</span><span class="p">));</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ResponseEntity</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">of</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">pd</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">build</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="nd">@ExceptionHandler</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">MethodArgumentNotValidException</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">class</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">ResponseEntity</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">ProblemDetail</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">invalid</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">MethodArgumentNotValidException</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ex</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">...</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Mention <strong>RFC 7807 <code>ProblemDetail</code></strong> (built into Spring 6) — it signals current knowledge.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-24"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q24</span><span class="qtext">Validation.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>@Valid</code>/<code>@Validated</code> + JSR-380: <code>@NotNull</code>, <code>@NotBlank</code>, <code>@Size</code>, <code>@Min/@Max</code>, <code>@Email</code>, <code>@Pattern</code>, <code>@Positive</code>, <code>@Past/@Future</code>, plus custom constraints (<code>@Constraint</code> + <code>ConstraintValidator</code>). <code>@Validated</code> on the class enables method-level validation and validation groups.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-25"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q25</span><span class="qtext">API versioning strategies.</span></p><div class="a"><p>URI (<code>/api/v1/bets</code> — simplest, most common), header (<code>Accept: application/vnd.company.v2+json</code> — purest), query param, content negotiation. Discuss backwards compatibility: additive changes only, tolerant reader, deprecation headers, sunset policy.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-26"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q26</span><span class="qtext">DTO vs Entity — why not expose the entity?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Leaks the schema, causes lazy-loading serialisation errors, couples the API to the DB, creates security holes (mass assignment), prevents independent evolution. Map with MapStruct (compile-time, fast) or manual mappers.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-27"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q27</span><span class="qtext"><code>RestTemplate</code> vs <code>WebClient</code> vs <code>RestClient</code> vs Feign.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>RestTemplate</code> — blocking, maintenance mode. <code>WebClient</code> — reactive, non-blocking, supports streaming, can be used with <code>.block()</code> but that's wasteful. <code>RestClient</code> (Spring 6.1) — synchronous with WebClient's fluent API; the modern blocking choice. <code>OpenFeign</code> — declarative interface-based client, integrates with Eureka/LoadBalancer/Resilience4j.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-28"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q28</span><span class="qtext">How do you secure a REST API?</span></p><div class="a"><p>JWT bearer tokens (or opaque tokens + introspection) with OAuth2/OIDC, HTTPS only, <code>@PreAuthorize</code> method security, CORS configured explicitly, rate limiting, input validation, no sensitive data in logs/URLs, short-lived access tokens + rotating refresh tokens.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-29"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q29</span><span class="qtext">JWT structure and pitfalls.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>header.payload.signature</code>, base64url encoded, signed (HS256 symmetric / RS256 asymmetric — prefer RS256 so services only need the public key).
Pitfalls: <strong>payload is not encrypted</strong>, can't be revoked before expiry (mitigate with short TTL + a denylist/<code>jti</code> in Redis), the <code>alg:none</code> attack, storing them in <code>localStorage</code> (XSS) vs httpOnly cookies (CSRF — use SameSite + CSRF token).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-30"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q30</span><span class="qtext">Spring Security filter chain.</span></p><div class="a"><p>A chain of servlet filters: <code>SecurityContextPersistenceFilter</code> → <code>CorsFilter</code> → <code>CsrfFilter</code> → authentication filters (<code>UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter</code>, <code>BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter</code>) → <code>ExceptionTranslationFilter</code> → <code>FilterSecurityInterceptor</code>/<code>AuthorizationFilter</code>. Modern config is the lambda DSL <code>SecurityFilterChain</code> bean; <code>WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter</code> is removed.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nd">@Bean</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">SecurityFilterChain</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">chain</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">HttpSecurity</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">http</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">throws</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Exception</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">http</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">csrf</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">csrf</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">csrf</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">disable</span><span class="p">())</span>
<span class="w">      </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">sessionManagement</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">s</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">s</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">sessionCreationPolicy</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">STATELESS</span><span class="p">))</span>
<span class="w">      </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">authorizeHttpRequests</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">a</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">a</span>
<span class="w">          </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">requestMatchers</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"/actuator/health"</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">permitAll</span><span class="p">()</span>
<span class="w">          </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">requestMatchers</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"/api/admin/**"</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">hasRole</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"ADMIN"</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">          </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">anyRequest</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">authenticated</span><span class="p">())</span>
<span class="w">      </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">oauth2ResourceServer</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">o</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">o</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">jwt</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Customizer</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">withDefaults</span><span class="p">()))</span>
<span class="w">      </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">build</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-31"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q31</span><span class="qtext">Authentication vs Authorization; RBAC.</span></p><div class="a"><p>AuthN = who you are; AuthZ = what you may do. <code>hasRole('ADMIN')</code> (prefix <code>ROLE_</code> added) vs <code>hasAuthority('bet:place')</code>. Prefer fine-grained authorities.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p2-4-jpa-hibernate">4. JPA / Hibernate<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p2-4-jpa-hibernate">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q2-32"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q32</span><span class="qtext">JPA vs Hibernate vs Spring Data JPA.</span></p><div class="a"><p>JPA = specification; Hibernate = the most common implementation; Spring Data JPA = a layer on top providing repositories, derived queries, paging, auditing.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-33"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q33</span><span class="qtext">Entity lifecycle states.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Transient (new, no ID, not in persistence context) → Managed/Persistent (tracked, dirty-checked, flushed at commit) → Detached (context closed) → Removed. <code>persist</code>, <code>merge</code> (returns a managed copy — the argument stays detached!), <code>remove</code>, <code>detach</code>, <code>refresh</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-34"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q34</span><span class="qtext"><code>getReference</code>/<code>getOne</code> vs <code>findById</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>findById</code> hits the DB and returns the entity or empty. <code>getReference</code> returns a <strong>lazy proxy</strong> without a query — good when you only need to set a FK; throws <code>EntityNotFoundException</code> on first access if absent.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-35"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q35</span><span class="qtext">First-level vs second-level cache.</span></p><div class="a"><p>L1 = the persistence context / <code>EntityManager</code>, always on, per-transaction, gives repeatable reads and identity guarantees. L2 = optional, per <code>SessionFactory</code>, shared across sessions (EhCache, Hazelcast, Caffeine, Infinispan), configured per entity with <code>@Cache</code>. Plus the query cache (needs L2, and is easy to get wrong).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-36"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q36</span><span class="qtext">LAZY vs EAGER; what is <code>LazyInitializationException</code>?</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>@ManyToOne</code> and <code>@OneToOne</code> default to <strong>EAGER</strong> (bad — always set them LAZY); <code>@OneToMany</code>/<code>@ManyToMany</code> default to LAZY. The exception occurs when you touch a lazy association after the session is closed (e.g. in the controller/serializer). Fixes: fetch what you need with <code>JOIN FETCH</code> or an <code>@EntityGraph</code>, project into a DTO, or use <code>@Transactional</code> at the right boundary. <strong>Do not</strong> use <code>spring.jpa.open-in-view=true</code> — turn it off; it holds connections for the whole request and hides N+1s.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-37"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q37</span><span class="qtext">The N+1 problem — how do you detect and fix it?</span></p><div class="a"><p>One query loads N parents, then N more fire for each child association.
<em>Detect:</em> enable <code>spring.jpa.show-sql</code> / <code>hibernate.generate_statistics</code>, use datasource-proxy or <strong>Hypersistence Utils' assertion</strong>, or p6spy; watch query counts in tests.
<em>Fix:</em></p><ul>
<li><code>@Query("select o from Order o join fetch o.items where ...")</code></li>
<li><code>@EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"items", "customer"})</code> on the repository method</li>
<li><code>@BatchSize(size = 25)</code> → turns N queries into N/25 <code>IN</code> queries</li>
<li>DTO projection (interface- or constructor-based) — best for read paths
⚠️ <code>JOIN FETCH</code> with pagination causes <code>HHH000104: firstResult/maxResults specified with collection fetch; applying in memory</code> — fix with a two-query approach (fetch IDs page, then fetch entities by IDs) or <code>@BatchSize</code>.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-38"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q38</span><span class="qtext"><code>FetchType</code> + <code>MultipleBagFetchException</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Fetching two <code>List</code> collections in one query throws it. Fix: change one to <code>Set</code>, or use two queries / <code>@BatchSize</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-39"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q39</span><span class="qtext">Optimistic vs pessimistic locking.</span></p><div class="a"><p><strong>Optimistic</strong> — <code>@Version</code> column; on update Hibernate adds <code>WHERE version = ?</code>; a mismatch throws <code>OptimisticLockException</code>/<code>ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException</code> → retry. Best for low contention and scales well (no DB locks held).
<strong>Pessimistic</strong> — <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code> via <code>@Lock(PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)</code>; blocks other writers. Use for high contention on a hot row (e.g. a wallet balance) and always set a lock timeout to avoid pile-ups.
<em>Entain answer:</em> for a user wallet debited by concurrent bets, I'd use either pessimistic locking on the balance row with a short timeout, or an append-only ledger with an atomic conditional update (<code>UPDATE wallet SET balance = balance - :amt WHERE id = :id AND balance &gt;= :amt</code>) and check the affected row count — which avoids read-modify-write entirely.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-40"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q40</span><span class="qtext">Cascade types and <code>orphanRemoval</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>PERSIST, MERGE, REMOVE, REFRESH, DETACH, ALL</code>. <code>orphanRemoval=true</code> deletes a child when removed from the parent collection (stronger than <code>CascadeType.REMOVE</code>). Never cascade REMOVE on <code>@ManyToMany</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-41"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q41</span><span class="qtext"><code>@OneToMany</code> mapping — who owns the relationship?</span></p><div class="a"><p>The side without <code>mappedBy</code> owns the FK. On a bidirectional association you must set both sides (a helper method <code>addItem()</code> doing <code>items.add(i); i.setOrder(this);</code>), otherwise the FK is null.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-42"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q42</span><span class="qtext">Inheritance strategies.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>SINGLE_TABLE</code> (fast, discriminator column, nullable columns), <code>JOINED</code> (normalised, joins on read), <code>TABLE_PER_CLASS</code> (unions, poor polymorphic queries), <code>@MappedSuperclass</code> (no polymorphic queries at all).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-43"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q43</span><span class="qtext">Derived queries, <code>@Query</code>, and Specifications.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>findByStatusAndCreatedAtAfterOrderByAmountDesc(...)</code>. <code>@Query</code> for JPQL/native (<code>nativeQuery = true</code>). <code>Specification&lt;T&gt;</code> / QueryDSL for dynamic criteria. Projections: interface-based (<code>interface OrderSummary { String getId(); BigDecimal getTotal(); }</code>) or <code>new com.x.Dto(...)</code> constructor expressions.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-44"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q44</span><span class="qtext">How do you handle DB schema migration?</span></p><div class="a"><p><strong>Flyway</strong> (SQL-first, versioned <code>V1__init.sql</code>, checksum-validated) or <strong>Liquibase</strong> (XML/YAML changelogs, rollback support, DB-agnostic). Never <code>hibernate.ddl-auto=update</code> in production — set it to <code>validate</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-45"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q45</span><span class="qtext">Connection pooling — HikariCP.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Default in Boot. Key settings: <code>maximum-pool-size</code> (usually 10–20; more is often <em>slower</em> — the DB is the bottleneck; sizing formula ≈ <code>cores × 2 + effective spindles</code>), <code>minimum-idle</code>, <code>connection-timeout</code>, <code>max-lifetime</code> (&lt; DB/network idle timeout), <code>leak-detection-threshold</code>. Symptom of exhaustion: <code>HikariPool-1 - Connection is not available, request timed out after 30000ms</code> — usually caused by long transactions, open-in-view, or calling an HTTP API inside a transaction.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p2-5-sql-databases">5. SQL &amp; Databases<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p2-5-sql-databases">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q2-46"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q46</span><span class="qtext">Joins.</span></p><div class="a"><p>INNER, LEFT/RIGHT OUTER, FULL OUTER, CROSS, SELF. Be ready to draw the Venn diagram and write a self-join for "employees and their managers".</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-47"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q47</span><span class="qtext">Common query questions.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="c1">-- Nth highest salary</span>
<span class="k">SELECT</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">DISTINCT</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">salary</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">FROM</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">employee</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">ORDER</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">BY</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">salary</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">DESC</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">OFFSET</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">ROWS</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">FETCH</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">NEXT</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">ROWS</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">ONLY</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="c1">-- or window function</span>
<span class="k">SELECT</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">*</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">FROM</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="k">SELECT</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">e</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="o">*</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">DENSE_RANK</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">OVER</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="k">ORDER</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">BY</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">salary</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">DESC</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">rnk</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">FROM</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">employee</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">e</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">t</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">WHERE</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">rnk</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">2</span><span class="p">;</span>

<span class="c1">-- Highest paid per department</span>
<span class="k">SELECT</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">*</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">FROM</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="k">SELECT</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">e</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="o">*</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ROW_NUMBER</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">OVER</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">PARTITION</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">BY</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">dept_id</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">ORDER</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">BY</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">salary</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">DESC</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">rn</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">FROM</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">employee</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">e</span>
<span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">t</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">WHERE</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">rn</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">;</span>

<span class="c1">-- Duplicates</span>
<span class="k">SELECT</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">email</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">COUNT</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="o">*</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">FROM</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">users</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">GROUP</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">BY</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">email</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">HAVING</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">COUNT</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="o">*</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">;</span>

<span class="c1">-- Delete duplicates keeping lowest id</span>
<span class="k">DELETE</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">FROM</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">users</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">a</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">USING</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">users</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">b</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">WHERE</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">a</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="n">id</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">b</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="n">id</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">AND</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">a</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="n">email</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">b</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="n">email</span><span class="p">;</span>

<span class="c1">-- Running total</span>
<span class="k">SELECT</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">id</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">amount</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">SUM</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">amount</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">OVER</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">PARTITION</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">BY</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">user_id</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">ORDER</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">BY</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">created_at</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">AS</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">running</span>
<span class="k">FROM</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">transactions</span><span class="p">;</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-48"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q48</span><span class="qtext"><code>WHERE</code> vs <code>HAVING</code>; <code>UNION</code> vs <code>UNION ALL</code>; <code>DELETE</code> vs <code>TRUNCATE</code> vs <code>DROP</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p>WHERE filters rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups after. UNION dedupes (sort cost), UNION ALL doesn't. DELETE is DML, row-by-row, logged, triggers fire, rollback-able; TRUNCATE is DDL, deallocates pages, resets identity, no triggers; DROP removes the table.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-49"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q49</span><span class="qtext">Indexes — how do they work, when do they hurt?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Usually a B-tree; turns O(n) scans into O(log n) seeks. Costs: slower INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE, storage, and the optimiser may still ignore them.</p><ul>
<li><strong>Composite index column order matters</strong> — the leftmost-prefix rule: an index on <code>(a, b, c)</code> supports <code>a</code>, <code>a,b</code>, <code>a,b,c</code> but not <code>b</code> alone.</li>
<li><strong>Covering index</strong> — includes all columns the query needs, so no table lookup ("index-only scan").</li>
<li>Index kills: functions on the column (<code>WHERE UPPER(name) = ...</code>), leading wildcard <code>LIKE '%x'</code>, implicit type conversions, <code>OR</code> across columns, low cardinality (a boolean).</li>
<li>Clustered (defines physical order, one per table) vs non-clustered.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-50"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q50</span><span class="qtext">How do you troubleshoot a slow query?</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> → look for seq scans on big tables, bad row estimates, nested loops over large sets, sorts spilling to disk. Then: add/adjust an index, rewrite to be sargable, reduce selected columns, avoid <code>SELECT *</code>, paginate with keyset (<code>WHERE id &gt; :last</code>) instead of large <code>OFFSET</code>, check statistics are fresh (<code>ANALYZE</code>), consider partitioning, denormalise or add a materialised view for reporting.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-51"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q51</span><span class="qtext">ACID.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Atomicity (all or nothing — undo log), Consistency (constraints hold), Isolation (concurrency control — MVCC/locks), Durability (WAL/redo log fsynced).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-52"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q52</span><span class="qtext">Normalisation vs denormalisation.</span></p><div class="a"><p>1NF atomic values, 2NF no partial dependency on part of a composite key, 3NF no transitive dependency, BCNF. Denormalise deliberately for read-heavy paths (reporting, feeds) and accept the write-side duplication cost.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-53"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q53</span><span class="qtext">SQL vs NoSQL — when would you pick which?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Relational for transactional integrity, complex joins, well-known schema (accounts, bets, orders). Document (MongoDB) for flexible schemas and aggregate-per-document access. Key-value (Redis) for cache/session/rate limiting/leaderboards. Wide-column (Cassandra) for massive write throughput and time-series with known access patterns. Search (Elasticsearch) for full-text and analytics. Graph (Neo4j) for relationship traversal (fraud rings).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-54"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q54</span><span class="qtext">Redis — what have you used it for?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Caching (cache-aside), distributed locks (<code>SET key val NX PX ttl</code> + Redlock caveats), rate limiting (token bucket / sliding window with sorted sets), session store, leaderboards (<code>ZSET</code> — natural fit for a betting leaderboard), pub/sub, idempotency keys. Discuss eviction policies (<code>allkeys-lru</code>), TTLs, and cache stampede protection (jittered TTL, mutex/single-flight, refresh-ahead).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-55"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q55</span><span class="qtext">Caching strategies.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Cache-aside (lazy loading — most common), read-through, write-through, write-behind, refresh-ahead. Problems: stale data, thundering herd, hot keys, cache penetration (cache the null), cache avalanche (stagger TTLs).
In Spring: <code>@EnableCaching</code>, <code>@Cacheable</code>, <code>@CachePut</code>, <code>@CacheEvict(allEntries=true)</code>, with Caffeine locally or Redis distributed. Note <code>@Cacheable</code> self-invocation has the same proxy limitation as <code>@Transactional</code>.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p2-6-microservices">6. Microservices<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p2-6-microservices">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q2-56"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q56</span><span class="qtext">Monolith vs microservices — when NOT to use microservices?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Small team, unclear domain boundaries, low scale, no DevOps maturity. Start with a modular monolith and extract when you have a real reason (independent scaling, independent deployment cadence, team autonomy, differing tech needs). Costs: distributed transactions, network failure modes, operational overhead, data consistency, debugging.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-57"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q57</span><span class="qtext">Core patterns you should be able to name and explain.</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li><strong>API Gateway</strong> (single entry, routing, auth, rate limiting, aggregation) — Spring Cloud Gateway.</li>
<li><strong>Service Discovery</strong> (Eureka, Consul, or K8s DNS/Service).</li>
<li><strong>Config Server</strong> / externalised configuration.</li>
<li><strong>Circuit Breaker / Bulkhead / Retry / Rate limiter</strong> — Resilience4j.</li>
<li><strong>Database per service</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Saga</strong> for distributed transactions.</li>
<li><strong>CQRS</strong> and <strong>Event Sourcing</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Outbox pattern</strong> for reliable event publishing.</li>
<li><strong>Strangler Fig</strong> for migration.</li>
<li><strong>Sidecar / Service Mesh</strong> (Istio, Envoy).</li>
<li><strong>Backend for Frontend (BFF)</strong>.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-58"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q58</span><span class="qtext">How do you handle distributed transactions? Explain Saga.</span></p><div class="a"><p>2PC is generally avoided (blocking, coordinator SPOF, poor scaling). <strong>Saga</strong> = a sequence of local transactions, each publishing an event; failures trigger <strong>compensating transactions</strong>.</p><ul>
<li><strong>Choreography</strong>: services react to each other's events. Simple for 2–3 services, but the flow becomes implicit and hard to trace.</li>
<li><strong>Orchestration</strong>: a central orchestrator (Camunda, Temporal, or a state machine service) issues commands. Explicit, testable, easier to monitor; slight coupling to the orchestrator.
<em>Example (Entain-flavoured):</em> PlaceBet → ReserveFunds → ValidateOdds → ConfirmBet. If ValidateOdds fails, emit <code>ReleaseFunds</code> as compensation. Compensations must be idempotent and can't assume the world is unchanged.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-59"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q59</span><span class="qtext">Outbox pattern — why do you need it?</span></p><div class="a"><p>You cannot atomically write to the DB and publish to Kafka. So write the event to an <code>outbox</code> table <strong>in the same local transaction</strong>, and have a relay (Debezium CDC on the WAL, or a polling publisher) push it to Kafka. Guarantees at-least-once delivery with no dual-write inconsistency. Consumers must therefore be idempotent.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-60"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q60</span><span class="qtext">Circuit breaker states and Resilience4j.</span></p><div class="a"><p>CLOSED → (failure rate threshold exceeded over a sliding window) → OPEN (fail fast, return fallback) → after <code>waitDurationInOpenState</code> → HALF_OPEN (limited trial calls) → CLOSED or back to OPEN.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nd">@CircuitBreaker</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">name</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"oddsService"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">fallbackMethod</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"cachedOdds"</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="nd">@Retry</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">name</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"oddsService"</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="nd">@Bulkhead</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">name</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"oddsService"</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="nd">@TimeLimiter</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">name</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"oddsService"</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Odds</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">getOdds</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">marketId</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">...</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>

<span class="kd">private</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Odds</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">cachedOdds</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">marketId</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Throwable</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">t</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">cache</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">get</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">marketId</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Order of annotations matters: Retry → CircuitBreaker → RateLimiter → TimeLimiter → Bulkhead (outer to inner). Retries must use <strong>exponential backoff with jitter</strong> and only for idempotent/transient failures, else you amplify the outage.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-61"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q61</span><span class="qtext">Distributed tracing / observability.</span></p><div class="a"><p>The three pillars: metrics (Micrometer → Prometheus → Grafana), logs (structured JSON, correlation ID in MDC, ELK/Loki), traces (Micrometer Tracing/OpenTelemetry → Jaeger/Zipkin, propagating W3C <code>traceparent</code>). Add RED metrics (Rate, Errors, Duration) and alert on SLOs, not raw CPU.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-62"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q62</span><span class="qtext">Inter-service communication: sync vs async.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Sync (REST/gRPC) — simple, immediate consistency, but temporal coupling and cascading failures. Async (Kafka/RabbitMQ) — decoupled, resilient, buffers load, but eventual consistency and harder debugging. Rule of thumb: <strong>commands that need an answer → sync; state changes others care about → events.</strong></p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-63"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q63</span><span class="qtext">gRPC vs REST.</span></p><div class="a"><p>gRPC: HTTP/2, Protobuf binary, strongly typed contracts, bidirectional streaming, much lower latency/payload — great for internal service-to-service. REST/JSON: human-readable, universal, browser-friendly, better for public APIs.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p2-7-kafka-messaging">7. Kafka &amp; Messaging<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p2-7-kafka-messaging">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q2-64"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q64</span><span class="qtext">Core Kafka concepts.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Topic → Partitions (unit of parallelism and ordering) → Segments. Producers pick a partition by key hash (<strong>same key → same partition → ordering guaranteed per key</strong>). Consumers form a <strong>consumer group</strong>; each partition is consumed by exactly one consumer in the group, so max useful parallelism = partition count. Offsets are committed per group per partition. Replication factor + ISR (in-sync replicas) for durability; a leader per partition.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-65"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q65</span><span class="qtext">Delivery semantics.</span></p><div class="a"><p>At-most-once (commit before processing), <strong>at-least-once</strong> (process then commit — the default and most common; requires idempotent consumers), exactly-once (Kafka transactions + <code>enable.idempotence=true</code> + <code>read_committed</code> — works only within Kafka; end-to-end EOS with an external DB still needs idempotency).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-66"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q66</span><span class="qtext">Producer config that matters.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>acks=all</code> + <code>min.insync.replicas=2</code> for durability, <code>enable.idempotence=true</code> (prevents duplicates from retries), <code>retries</code>, <code>linger.ms</code> + <code>batch.size</code> for throughput, <code>compression.type=snappy/lz4</code>, <code>max.in.flight.requests.per.connection=5</code> (safe with idempotence).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-67"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q67</span><span class="qtext">Consumer config that matters.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>auto.offset.reset</code> (earliest/latest), <code>enable.auto.commit=false</code> + manual ack for correctness, <code>max.poll.records</code> and <code>max.poll.interval.ms</code> (too-slow processing → rebalance storms), <code>session.timeout.ms</code>/<code>heartbeat.interval.ms</code>, <code>isolation.level</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-68"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q68</span><span class="qtext">How do you handle a poison message / failing consumer?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Retry topics with backoff + a <strong>Dead Letter Topic</strong>. In Spring Kafka: <code>DefaultErrorHandler</code> with <code>ExponentialBackOff</code> + <code>DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer</code>. Classify exceptions: retryable (transient IO) vs non-retryable (deserialisation, validation → straight to DLT). Then monitor and have a replay tool.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-69"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q69</span><span class="qtext">How do you guarantee ordering, and what breaks it?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Ordering is only per partition. Broken by: multiple partitions, retries with in-flight &gt; 1 without idempotence, concurrent consumer threads within a partition, and re-keying. If you need per-entity ordering, key by entity ID (e.g. <code>userId</code> or <code>betId</code>).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-70"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q70</span><span class="qtext">Kafka vs RabbitMQ.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Kafka: distributed log, retained/replayable, pull-based, huge throughput, ordering per partition, stream processing. RabbitMQ: broker with flexible routing (exchanges, topics, fanout), push-based, per-message ack and priorities, good for task queues and RPC. Choose Kafka for event streaming/audit/replay; Rabbit for complex routing and job dispatch.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-71"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q71</span><span class="qtext">Consumer lag — what is it and how do you fix it?</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>log-end-offset - committed-offset</code>. Fix by adding partitions + consumers, increasing <code>max.poll.records</code>/parallelism inside the consumer, making processing faster (batch DB writes), or moving heavy work off the consumer thread. Alert on lag trend, not absolute value.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p2-8-testing-code-quality">8. Testing &amp; Code Quality<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p2-8-testing-code-quality">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q2-72"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q72</span><span class="qtext">Testing pyramid.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Many fast unit tests → fewer integration tests → very few E2E. Plus contract tests (Spring Cloud Contract / Pact) between services.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-73"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q73</span><span class="qtext">JUnit 5 + Mockito essentials.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nd">@ExtendWith</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">MockitoExtension</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">class</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">BetServiceTest</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="nd">@Mock</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">WalletClient</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">wallet</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="nd">@Mock</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BetRepository</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">repo</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="nd">@InjectMocks</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BetService</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">service</span><span class="p">;</span>

<span class="w">    </span><span class="nd">@Test</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">void</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">rejectsBetWhenBalanceInsufficient</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">when</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">wallet</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">balance</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"u1"</span><span class="p">)).</span><span class="na">thenReturn</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BigDecimal</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"5.00"</span><span class="p">));</span>

<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">assertThatThrownBy</span><span class="p">(()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">service</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">place</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BetRequest</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"u1"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BigDecimal</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"10.00"</span><span class="p">))))</span>
<span class="w">            </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">isInstanceOf</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">InsufficientBalanceException</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">class</span><span class="p">);</span>

<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">verify</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">repo</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">never</span><span class="p">()).</span><span class="na">save</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">any</span><span class="p">());</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">verifyNoMoreInteractions</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">repo</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Know: <code>@BeforeEach/@AfterEach/@BeforeAll</code>, <code>@ParameterizedTest</code> + <code>@CsvSource</code>/<code>@MethodSource</code>, <code>@Nested</code>, <code>@DisplayName</code>, <code>assertThrows</code>, <code>ArgumentCaptor</code>, <code>doThrow().when()</code> for voids, <code>@Spy</code> for partial mocks, <code>mockStatic</code> for static methods.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-74"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q74</span><span class="qtext">Spring test slices.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>@SpringBootTest</code> (full context — slow), <code>@WebMvcTest</code> (controllers + MockMvc, service mocked with <code>@MockBean</code>), <code>@DataJpaTest</code> (in-memory or Testcontainers DB, transactional and rolled back), <code>@RestClientTest</code>, <code>@JsonTest</code>. <strong>Testcontainers</strong> for real Postgres/Kafka in integration tests — mention this, it's a strong signal.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-75"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q75</span><span class="qtext">What makes a good unit test?</span></p><div class="a"><p>FIRST: Fast, Independent, Repeatable, Self-validating, Timely. Arrange–Act–Assert, one logical assertion, behaviour not implementation, deterministic (inject a <code>Clock</code>, don't call <code>Instant.now()</code> directly), no sleeps.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-76"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q76</span><span class="qtext">How do you measure and enforce quality?</span></p><div class="a"><p>JaCoCo coverage gates (but coverage ≠ quality), SonarQube (bugs, code smells, duplication, security hotspots), Checkstyle/Spotless, SpotBugs, dependency scanning (OWASP Dependency-Check, Snyk), mutation testing (PIT) as the honest coverage metric, mandatory PR review, CI gates on the build.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q2-77"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q77</span><span class="qtext">Code review — what do you look for?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Correctness and edge cases, test coverage of the new behaviour, naming/readability, SOLID violations, error handling and logging (no swallowed exceptions, no secrets logged), N+1 and transaction boundaries, thread-safety, backwards compatibility of APIs and DB migrations, security (injection, authz checks), and whether it's the simplest thing that works.</p></div></section><hr/>
<p><em>Next: Part 3 — Design Patterns, SOLID, System Design.</em></p></div></section>
<section class="panel" id="panel-3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-3" hidden><header class="part-head"><span class="part-n">Part 03</span><h1>SOLID, Patterns &amp; System Design</h1><p class="kicker">Judgement and structure <span class="dot">·</span> 2 questions</p></header><div class="prose">
<blockquote>
<p>This is the section that separates a 5-year candidate from a 2-year one. Both EPAM and Entain will probe it. EPAM in particular loves clean-code/patterns discussion; Entain will push system design under load.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p3-1-solid-know-the-definition-a-violation-and-the-fix-for-each">1. SOLID (know the definition, a violation, and the fix for each)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-1-solid-know-the-definition-a-violation-and-the-fix-for-each">#</a></h2>
<h3 id="p3-s-single-responsibility-principle">S — Single Responsibility Principle<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-s-single-responsibility-principle">#</a></h3>
<p><em>A class should have one reason to change.</em></p>
<p>❌ Violation: <code>OrderService</code> that validates, persists, sends email, and generates a PDF invoice. A change to the email provider forces a change to order logic.
✅ Fix: <code>OrderValidator</code>, <code>OrderRepository</code>, <code>NotificationService</code>, <code>InvoiceGenerator</code>, with <code>OrderService</code> orchestrating.
<em>Signals of violation:</em> the class name has "And" or "Manager"/"Util", the test needs six mocks, unrelated developers keep touching the same file.</p>
<h3 id="p3-o-open-closed-principle">O — Open/Closed Principle<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-o-open-closed-principle">#</a></h3>
<p><em>Open for extension, closed for modification.</em></p>
<p>❌ Violation:</p>
<div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">BigDecimal</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">fee</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Payment</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">p</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">if</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">p</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">type</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">==</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">CARD</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">...;</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">else</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">if</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">p</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">type</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">==</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">PAYPAL</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">...;</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// every new type edits this method</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div>
<p>✅ Fix — Strategy + polymorphism:</p>
<div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">interface</span> <span class="nc">FeeCalculator</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">boolean</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">supports</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">PaymentType</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">t</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BigDecimal</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">fee</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Payment</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">p</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>

<span class="nd">@Service</span>
<span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">FeeService</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">private</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">final</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Map</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">PaymentType</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">FeeCalculator</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">byType</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">FeeService</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">FeeCalculator</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">calculators</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// Spring injects all impls</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="k">this</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">byType</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">calculators</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">toMap</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">FeeCalculator</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">type</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">identity</span><span class="p">()));</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BigDecimal</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">fee</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Payment</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">p</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">byType</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">getOrDefault</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">p</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">type</span><span class="p">(),</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">FeeCalculator</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">NOOP</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">fee</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">p</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div>
<p>Adding PaymentType.CRYPTO = add one class, change nothing else.</p>
<h3 id="p3-l-liskov-substitution-principle">L — Liskov Substitution Principle<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-l-liskov-substitution-principle">#</a></h3>
<p><em>Subtypes must be substitutable for their base type without breaking correctness.</em></p>
<p>❌ Classic violation: <code>Square extends Rectangle</code> — <code>setWidth</code> also changing height breaks a caller that assumes independence. Or <code>ReadOnlyList extends List</code> throwing <code>UnsupportedOperationException</code> from <code>add</code>.
Rules: a subclass may <strong>weaken</strong> preconditions and <strong>strengthen</strong> postconditions, never the reverse; must not throw new exception types; must preserve invariants.
✅ Fix: prefer composition, or model the shared abstraction correctly (<code>Shape</code> with <code>area()</code>).</p>
<h3 id="p3-i-interface-segregation-principle">I — Interface Segregation Principle<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-i-interface-segregation-principle">#</a></h3>
<p><em>No client should be forced to depend on methods it doesn't use.</em></p>
<p>❌ <code>interface Worker { void work(); void eat(); void attendMeeting(); }</code> implemented by <code>RobotWorker</code> which throws on <code>eat()</code>.
✅ Split into <code>Workable</code>, <code>Feedable</code>. In practice: many small role interfaces (<code>OddsProvider</code>, <code>BetValidator</code>) instead of one fat <code>BettingService</code> interface.</p>
<h3 id="p3-d-dependency-inversion-principle">D — Dependency Inversion Principle<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-d-dependency-inversion-principle">#</a></h3>
<p><em>High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both depend on abstractions.</em></p>
<p>❌ <code>OrderService</code> doing <code>new MySqlOrderRepository()</code>.
✅ <code>OrderService</code> depends on the <code>OrderRepository</code> interface, injected by Spring. Crucially, <strong>the interface belongs to the high-level module</strong> (the domain), and the adapter implements it — that's the inversion, and it's exactly what Hexagonal Architecture formalises.</p>
<p><strong>Other principles worth naming:</strong> DRY, KISS, YAGNI, Law of Demeter ("don't talk to strangers" — <code>a.getB().getC().doThing()</code> is a smell), Composition over Inheritance, Tell-Don't-Ask, Fail Fast, Principle of Least Astonishment.</p>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p3-2-design-patterns">2. Design Patterns<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-2-design-patterns">#</a></h2>
<h3 id="p3-creational">Creational<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-creational">#</a></h3>
<p><strong>Singleton</strong> — one instance globally.</p>
<div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">enum</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ConfigCache</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">INSTANCE</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w">        </span><span class="c1">// best: serialization &amp; reflection safe</span>
</code></pre></div>
<p>Thread-safe variants: eager, static holder idiom (<code>private static class Holder { static final X I = new X(); }</code> — lazy + thread-safe with no locking), double-checked locking with <code>volatile</code>. In Spring you just use a singleton bean. Downside: global state, hard to test — often an anti-pattern.</p>
<p><strong>Factory Method</strong> — subclass decides which concrete class to create.</p>
<div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">interface</span> <span class="nc">NotificationFactory</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Notification</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">create</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div>
<p>Used in JDK: <code>Calendar.getInstance()</code>, <code>NumberFormat.getInstance()</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Abstract Factory</strong> — a factory of related factories, producing families of objects (e.g. <code>UiFactory</code> → <code>WindowsButton</code>+<code>WindowsCheckbox</code> vs Mac variants). JDK: <code>DocumentBuilderFactory</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Builder</strong> — step-by-step construction of complex/immutable objects with many optional params; avoids telescoping constructors.</p>
<div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">Bet</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">bet</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Bet</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">builder</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">userId</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"u1"</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">marketId</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"m9"</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">             </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stake</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BigDecimal</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"10.00"</span><span class="p">)).</span><span class="na">odds</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BigDecimal</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"2.5"</span><span class="p">))</span>
<span class="w">             </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">placedAt</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Instant</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">now</span><span class="p">()).</span><span class="na">build</span><span class="p">();</span>
</code></pre></div>
<p>JDK: <code>StringBuilder</code>, <code>Stream.Builder</code>, <code>HttpRequest.newBuilder()</code>. Lombok <code>@Builder</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Prototype</strong> — clone an existing instance instead of building from scratch (expensive initialisation). <code>Object.clone()</code>, or a copy constructor.</p>
<p><strong>Object Pool</strong> — reuse expensive objects: HikariCP connections, thread pools.</p>
<h3 id="p3-structural">Structural<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-structural">#</a></h3>
<p><strong>Adapter</strong> — make an incompatible interface usable. <code>InputStreamReader</code> adapts a byte stream to a char stream. Real use: wrapping a legacy/third-party payment SDK behind your own <code>PaymentGateway</code> interface.</p>
<p><strong>Decorator</strong> — add behaviour dynamically by wrapping, without subclass explosion.</p>
<div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">Reader</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">r</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">BufferedReader</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">InputStreamReader</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">FileInputStream</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">f</span><span class="p">)));</span>
</code></pre></div>
<p>Real use: adding caching/logging/retry around a repository. <code>Collections.unmodifiableList</code> is a decorator.</p>
<p><strong>Proxy</strong> — a stand-in controlling access. Types: virtual (lazy loading — Hibernate proxies), protection (security), remote (RMI/Feign), smart. <strong>Spring AOP, <code>@Transactional</code>, <code>@Cacheable</code> are all proxies.</strong>
<em>Decorator vs Proxy:</em> decorator adds behaviour and you compose many; proxy controls access and typically manages the lifecycle of exactly one target.</p>
<p><strong>Facade</strong> — a simplified entry point over a complex subsystem. Your <code>@Service</code> layer over repositories + clients is effectively a facade.</p>
<p><strong>Composite</strong> — treat individual objects and compositions uniformly (tree structures: file system, UI components, nested bet selections in an accumulator).</p>
<p><strong>Bridge</strong> — decouple abstraction from implementation so both vary independently. JDBC: <code>Driver</code> interface vs vendor drivers.</p>
<p><strong>Flyweight</strong> — share immutable intrinsic state to save memory. <code>Integer.valueOf</code> cache (-128..127), String pool.</p>
<h3 id="p3-behavioural">Behavioural<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-behavioural">#</a></h3>
<p><strong>Strategy</strong> — interchangeable algorithms. <em>Most-used pattern in real Spring code.</em> (See OCP example above.)</p>
<p><strong>Observer</strong> — publish/subscribe; one-to-many notification. Spring's <code>ApplicationEventPublisher</code> + <code>@EventListener</code> / <code>@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)</code> — the latter is the correct way to publish an event only after the DB commit.</p>
<p><strong>Template Method</strong> — skeleton in the base class, steps overridden by subclasses. <code>AbstractList</code>, <code>JdbcTemplate</code>, <code>HttpServlet.service()</code>.</p>
<div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">abstract</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">ImportJob</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">final</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">void</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">run</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">var</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">d</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">extract</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">var</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">t</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">transform</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">d</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">load</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">t</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w">  </span><span class="c1">// final = skeleton</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">protected</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">abstract</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Row</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">extract</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">protected</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Row</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">transform</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Row</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">in</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">in</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// hook with default</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">protected</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">abstract</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">void</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">load</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Row</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">rows</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>Chain of Responsibility</strong> — pass a request along handlers until one handles it. Servlet filters, Spring Security filter chain, Netty pipelines. Great answer for a fraud/validation rules engine.</p>
<p><strong>Command</strong> — encapsulate a request as an object (undo, queueing, logging). <code>Runnable</code>, and every CQRS command.</p>
<p><strong>State</strong> — object changes behaviour when internal state changes; replaces sprawling if/else on status. A <code>Bet</code> moving PLACED → MATCHED → SETTLED → PAID.</p>
<p><strong>Iterator</strong> — sequential access without exposing internals. <code>Iterator</code>/<code>Iterable</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Mediator</strong> — centralise complex communication (a Saga orchestrator).</p>
<p><strong>Memento</strong> — capture and restore state (undo, snapshots).</p>
<p><strong>Visitor</strong> — add operations to an object structure without modifying it (double dispatch). Good with sealed classes + pattern matching in modern Java.</p>
<p><strong>Interpreter / Null Object / Specification</strong> — Null Object avoids null checks; Specification composes business rules (<code>spec.and(other)</code>) and pairs with Spring Data <code>Specification</code>.</p>
<h3 id="p3-architectural-enterprise-patterns">Architectural / Enterprise patterns<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-architectural-enterprise-patterns">#</a></h3>
<p>DTO, Repository, Unit of Work, Service Layer, Dependency Injection, MVC, Front Controller (<code>DispatcherServlet</code>), Data Mapper vs Active Record, CQRS, Event Sourcing, Saga, Outbox, Circuit Breaker, Bulkhead, Sidecar, Ambassador, Strangler Fig, Anti-Corruption Layer.</p>
<section class="qa" id="q3-1"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q</span><span class="qtext">Which patterns have you actually used?</span></p><div class="a"><p>— Have 3 concrete stories ready. A strong answer: <em>"Strategy + a Spring-injected <code>List&lt;Handler&gt;</code> to replace a growing switch on promotion types; Chain of Responsibility for the bet-validation rule pipeline; Builder for immutable request objects; Template Method for a family of nightly reconciliation jobs; and the Outbox pattern to publish settlement events reliably."</em></p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q3-2"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q</span><span class="qtext">Patterns in the Spring/JDK you can point at.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Singleton (beans), Factory (<code>BeanFactory</code>), Proxy (AOP/transactions), Template Method (<code>JdbcTemplate</code>, <code>RestTemplate</code>), Front Controller (<code>DispatcherServlet</code>), MVC, Observer (application events), Decorator (<code>BufferedReader</code>), Adapter (<code>HandlerAdapter</code>), Builder (<code>UriComponentsBuilder</code>), Strategy (<code>Resource</code> implementations, <code>HandlerMapping</code>).</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p3-3-architecture-styles">3. Architecture Styles<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-3-architecture-styles">#</a></h2>
<p><strong>Layered (n-tier):</strong> Controller → Service → Repository → DB. Simple, familiar, but the domain ends up anaemic and depends on infrastructure.</p>
<p><strong>Hexagonal / Ports &amp; Adapters (Clean Architecture):</strong> the domain is at the centre with no framework dependencies; <strong>ports</strong> are interfaces owned by the domain; <strong>adapters</strong> (REST controller, JPA repository, Kafka consumer) implement/drive them. Dependencies point inward only. Benefits: testability without Spring, swappable infrastructure, business rules isolated. Structure it as <code>domain/</code>, <code>application/</code> (use cases), <code>adapter/in/</code>, <code>adapter/out/</code>.</p>
<p><strong>DDD vocabulary you should be able to use:</strong> ubiquitous language, bounded context, aggregate + aggregate root (the transaction boundary and consistency boundary), entity vs value object, domain event, repository per aggregate, anti-corruption layer, context map. A very good micro-answer: <em>"Service boundaries should follow bounded contexts, and one aggregate = one transaction — cross-aggregate consistency goes through events."</em></p>
<p><strong>CQRS:</strong> separate the write model (normalised, validated, aggregate-based) from the read model (denormalised, query-optimised, possibly a different store), synchronised by events. Use when read and write loads/shapes differ wildly. Cost: eventual consistency and more moving parts.</p>
<p><strong>Event Sourcing:</strong> store the sequence of events as the source of truth rather than current state; rebuild by replay; snapshots for performance. Gives a full audit trail (attractive for regulated betting/finance) but makes queries, schema evolution and GDPR deletion hard.</p>
<p><strong>Event-driven architecture:</strong> events vs commands, event notification vs event-carried state transfer, eventual consistency, idempotent consumers, ordering.</p>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p3-4-system-design-the-framework">4. System Design — the framework<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-4-system-design-the-framework">#</a></h2>
<p>Use this structure out loud in every design question. Interviewers score the process as much as the answer.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Clarify requirements</strong> (2–3 min). Functional. Non-functional: scale (DAU, RPS, peak multiplier), latency SLA (p99), consistency vs availability, durability, retention, read:write ratio, regions.</li>
<li><strong>Back-of-envelope estimates.</strong> e.g. 10M DAU, 10 requests/day = 100M req/day ≈ <strong>1,160 RPS average, ~5–10k peak</strong>. Storage: 1KB/record × 100M/day = 100GB/day = 36TB/yr. Memory for cache: 20% hot × ... Keep the numbers round.</li>
<li><strong>API design.</strong> Define the 3–5 endpoints/events with request/response shape.</li>
<li><strong>Data model.</strong> Tables/collections, keys, indexes, partition key choice.</li>
<li><strong>High-level architecture.</strong> Client → CDN → LB → API Gateway → services → cache → DB → async workers → queue.</li>
<li><strong>Deep dive</strong> on the 1–2 hard parts (the interviewer will steer).</li>
<li><strong>Scale &amp; bottlenecks.</strong> Caching layers, read replicas, sharding, partitioning, async processing, CDN, connection pools.</li>
<li><strong>Reliability &amp; operations.</strong> Failure modes, retries/idempotency, circuit breakers, monitoring, alerting, deployment strategy, DR/RPO/RTO.</li>
<li><strong>Trade-offs.</strong> State what you gave up and why.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Key concepts to have crisp:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>CAP:</strong> under a network partition you choose consistency or availability. Realistically it's <strong>PACELC</strong> — else, latency vs consistency. Most systems are AP with tuneable consistency; money movement is CP.</li>
<li><strong>Scaling:</strong> vertical vs horizontal; stateless services scale horizontally.</li>
<li><strong>Load balancing:</strong> L4 vs L7, round-robin/least-connections/consistent hashing, health checks, sticky sessions (avoid — externalise session state).</li>
<li><strong>Sharding:</strong> by hash (even, but resharding is painful), by range (hot spots), by geography, or directory-based. <strong>Consistent hashing</strong> minimises movement when nodes change.</li>
<li><strong>Replication:</strong> leader-follower (read scaling, replica lag → read-your-writes issues), multi-leader, leaderless (quorum R+W&gt;N).</li>
<li><strong>Rate limiting:</strong> token bucket (allows bursts), leaky bucket, fixed window (boundary spike problem), <strong>sliding window log/counter</strong>. Distributed via Redis with an atomic Lua script.</li>
<li><strong>Idempotency</strong>, <strong>exactly-once illusion</strong>, <strong>at-least-once + dedupe</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Backpressure</strong>, bounded queues, load shedding, graceful degradation.</li>
<li><strong>Bloom filter</strong> to avoid pointless DB lookups; <strong>consistent hashing</strong> for cache clusters.</li>
</ul>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p3-5-worked-designs-rehearse-these-out-loud">5. Worked designs (rehearse these out loud)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-5-worked-designs-rehearse-these-out-loud">#</a></h2>
<h3 id="p3-a-url-shortener-the-warm-up">A. URL Shortener (the warm-up)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-a-url-shortener-the-warm-up">#</a></h3>
<ul>
<li>Requirements: shorten, redirect, custom alias, expiry, analytics. 100M writes/day → ~1,160 write RPS, 100:1 read ratio → ~116k read RPS.</li>
<li>Key generation: base62 of a distributed counter (Snowflake/Zookeeper range allocation) — collision-free and short; or hash + collision retry. 7 chars of base62 = 3.5 trillion URLs.</li>
<li>Storage: KV store (<code>shortCode → longUrl, userId, expiry</code>). ~36TB/yr, so shard by shortCode hash.</li>
<li>Read path: CDN/edge cache → Redis (hot 20%) → DB. Return <strong>301</strong> for permanent (cacheable, loses analytics) or <strong>302</strong> if you need per-click tracking.</li>
<li>Analytics: fire an event to Kafka, aggregate asynchronously — never write to the DB on the redirect path.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="p3-b-rate-limiter-very-common-ties-to-the-api-gateway">B. Rate Limiter (very common; ties to the API gateway)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-b-rate-limiter-very-common-ties-to-the-api-gateway">#</a></h3>
<ul>
<li>Per-user + per-IP + per-endpoint limits, distributed across N app instances.</li>
<li>Algorithm: sliding window counter in Redis. Atomic Lua script does <code>INCR</code> on a windowed key + <code>EXPIRE</code>, or a <code>ZSET</code> of timestamps with <code>ZREMRANGEBYSCORE</code>.</li>
<li>Return <strong>429</strong> with <code>Retry-After</code> and <code>X-RateLimit-Remaining</code> headers.</li>
<li>Trade-offs: Redis is a SPOF (cluster + local fallback with a permissive default), slight over-admission with approximate counting. Discuss doing it at the gateway vs in each service.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="p3-c-notification-service-fan-out-retries-multi-channel">C. Notification Service (fan-out, retries, multi-channel)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-c-notification-service-fan-out-retries-multi-channel">#</a></h3>
<ul>
<li>API accepts a notification request → validates → writes to DB + Kafka (<strong>outbox pattern</strong>).</li>
<li>Channel workers (email/SMS/push) consume, each with its own topic and rate limits per provider.</li>
<li>Retries with exponential backoff → DLQ after N attempts. Idempotency by <code>notificationId</code> so a redelivery doesn't double-send.</li>
<li>User preference service, quiet hours, template service, priority queues.</li>
<li>Fan-out for 1M users: batch, chunk, and parallelise; don't hold it in one transaction.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="p3-d-e-commerce-order-checkout-epam-s-favourite-shape">D. E-commerce Order / Checkout (EPAM's favourite shape)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-d-e-commerce-order-checkout-epam-s-favourite-shape">#</a></h3>
<ul>
<li>Services: Catalog, Cart, Inventory, Order, Payment, Shipping, Notification.</li>
<li>Saga (orchestrated): CreateOrder(PENDING) → ReserveInventory → ChargePayment → ConfirmOrder → ship. Compensations: ReleaseInventory, RefundPayment.</li>
<li><strong>Inventory oversell</strong> is the hard part: reserve with an atomic conditional update (<code>UPDATE stock SET qty = qty - 1 WHERE sku = ? AND qty &gt;= 1</code>) and check the row count, plus a reservation TTL so abandoned carts release stock.</li>
<li>Idempotency-Key on checkout so a double-click doesn't create two orders.</li>
<li>Read path: catalog in Elasticsearch + Redis; write path in Postgres.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="p3-e-betting-wagering-platform-rehearse-this-specifically-for-e">E. Betting / Wagering Platform ⭐ (rehearse this specifically for Entain)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-e-betting-wagering-platform-rehearse-this-specifically-for-e">#</a></h3>
<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> place a bet on a market, real-time odds, wallet debit, bet settlement after the event, cash-out, limits and responsible-gambling checks, full auditability, regulatory reporting.</p>
<p><strong>Non-functional:</strong> spikes at event kickoff (10–100× normal), sub-100ms placement p99, <strong>no double-debits</strong>, no bets accepted at stale odds, complete audit trail, multi-jurisdiction rules.</p>
<p><strong>Architecture:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Odds/Feed Service</strong> — consumes provider feeds, publishes odds updates to Kafka keyed by <code>marketId</code>; pushes to clients via WebSocket; keeps the latest odds in Redis.</li>
<li><strong>Bet Placement Service</strong> — the hot path. Validates: market open, odds still valid (client sends the odds it saw; reject with 409 if moved beyond tolerance), stake within user/market limits, responsible-gambling and self-exclusion checks, KYC status.</li>
<li><strong>Wallet Service</strong> — the money. Append-only <strong>ledger</strong> (double-entry: debit user, credit house-liability) rather than mutating a balance; balance is a projection. Atomic conditional debit or pessimistic lock on the balance row. <code>Idempotency-Key</code> per bet so retries can't double-debit.</li>
<li><strong>Bet Store</strong> — bet is written with status PLACED, then an event <code>BetPlaced</code> via the outbox.</li>
<li><strong>Settlement Service</strong> — consumes results, matches bets by market/selection, computes payouts, credits wallets, emits <code>BetSettled</code>. Must be idempotent and replayable (results get corrected/voided).</li>
<li><strong>Risk/Trading Service</strong> — aggregates exposure per market in near real time, triggers odds adjustment or suspension.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Hard parts to raise unprompted (this is what impresses):</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stale odds</strong> — optimistic check on odds version; suspend the market during a price change; accept/reject rules per jurisdiction.</li>
<li><strong>Concurrency on the wallet</strong> — one aggregate = one transaction; ledger + conditional update avoids lost updates; never read-then-write.</li>
<li><strong>Exactly-once settlement</strong> — at-least-once Kafka delivery + a dedupe table on <code>(betId, settlementRunId)</code> with a unique constraint.</li>
<li><strong>Spike handling</strong> — bet placement is written to a durable queue and acknowledged fast, or the service is scaled horizontally with a bounded thread pool + load shedding; Redis for odds/limits so the hot path avoids the DB.</li>
<li><strong>Consistency choice</strong> — the wallet is CP (correctness over availability); odds display is AP (stale odds shown briefly is acceptable, stale odds <em>accepted</em> is not).</li>
<li><strong>Auditability</strong> — event sourcing or an immutable ledger + append-only bet history; regulators require reconstructing any bet's state at any point in time.</li>
<li><strong>Responsible gambling</strong> — deposit/loss/time limits enforced synchronously before acceptance, self-exclusion list checked on every placement.</li>
</ol>
<h3 id="p3-f-real-time-chat-websocket-feed">F. Real-time Chat / WebSocket feed<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-f-real-time-chat-websocket-feed">#</a></h3>
<ul>
<li>WebSocket (or SSE for one-way) connections held by a stateless gateway; connection registry in Redis mapping <code>userId → gatewayNode</code>; messages routed via Kafka/Redis pub-sub between nodes. Heartbeats, reconnect with a resume token, backpressure on slow clients, sticky routing at the LB.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="p3-g-distributed-job-scheduler-cron-in-a-cluster">G. Distributed job scheduler / cron in a cluster<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-g-distributed-job-scheduler-cron-in-a-cluster">#</a></h3>
<ul>
<li>Problem: N instances, the job should run once. Solutions: ShedLock (DB/Redis lock), Quartz clustered mode, a leader-election (K8s lease/Zookeeper), or an external scheduler triggering an endpoint. Discuss missed runs, overlapping executions, and idempotency.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="p3-h-log-metrics-ingestion-pipeline">H. Log/metrics ingestion pipeline<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-h-log-metrics-ingestion-pipeline">#</a></h3>
<ul>
<li>Agents → Kafka (partition by service) → stream processor (Flink/Kafka Streams) → hot store (Elasticsearch, 7 days) + cold store (S3/Parquet). Discuss retention tiers, sampling, cardinality explosion in metrics labels.</li>
</ul>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p3-6-likely-follow-up-how-would-you-handle-x-questions">6. Likely follow-up "how would you handle X" questions<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p3-6-likely-follow-up-how-would-you-handle-x-questions">#</a></h2>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>"Your API's p99 latency jumped from 80ms to 2s. Walk me through it."</strong>
  → Check the change log / recent deploys. Look at dashboards: is it all endpoints or one? CPU/GC (long GC pauses?), thread pool saturation, DB connection pool wait time, downstream dependency latency, cache hit rate drop, a slow query from a new index-less column, a noisy neighbour, or increased traffic. Use traces to find which span grew. Mitigate first (rollback/scale/circuit-break), then root-cause.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>"Two users buy the last item / place a bet at the same time."</strong> → Optimistic locking with a version column and retry, or a conditional atomic UPDATE checking the row count, or pessimistic lock with timeout. Explain why the naive read-then-write loses updates.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>"How do you do zero-downtime deploys with a DB change?"</strong> → Expand-contract: add the nullable column → deploy code writing both → backfill → deploy code reading new → drop the old column in a later release. Never a breaking migration in one step. Blue/green or canary at the service level, backwards-compatible APIs.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>"How do you handle a downstream service that's down?"</strong> → Circuit breaker + fallback (cached/default value), timeouts on every call (never infinite), bulkhead so it can't exhaust the whole thread pool, retry only idempotent calls with jittered backoff, degrade the feature gracefully, alert.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>"How do you migrate a monolith to microservices?"</strong> → Strangler Fig: put a facade/gateway in front, identify bounded contexts, extract the least-coupled/highest-value service first, use an anti-corruption layer, split the data last (dual-write → CDC → cut over), keep the ability to roll back.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>"How would you improve throughput of a batch job processing 10M rows?"</strong> → Chunked reads with keyset pagination, batch inserts (<code>rewriteBatchedStatements</code>, JDBC batch size), parallel partitions by key range, disable per-row Hibernate overhead (use JdbcTemplate or Spring Batch), tune commit interval, index only what's needed, run off-peak, make it restartable.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr/>
<p><em>Next: Part 4 — Frontend (React/Angular) for EPAM + DevOps.</em></p></div></section>
<section class="panel" id="panel-4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-4" hidden><header class="part-head"><span class="part-n">Part 04</span><h1>React, Angular &amp; DevOps</h1><p class="kicker">EPAM full-stack round <span class="dot">·</span> 70 questions</p></header><div class="prose">
<blockquote>
<p><strong>EPAM-critical</strong> (their round is 20% frontend, but they will test both React <em>and</em> Angular since the JD names both). Entain may ask a couple of light JS questions only.
Strategy for EPAM: be genuinely strong in one, competent in the other, and honest about which. Saying <em>"I've shipped production React; on Angular I've worked on modules with RxJS and forms and here's how it differs"</em> is far better than bluffing.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p4-1-javascript-core">1. JavaScript Core<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p4-1-javascript-core">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q4-1"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q1</span><span class="qtext"><code>var</code> vs <code>let</code> vs <code>const</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>var</code> — function-scoped, hoisted and initialised to <code>undefined</code>, re-declarable, attaches to <code>window</code> at top level. <code>let</code>/<code>const</code> — block-scoped, hoisted but in the <strong>temporal dead zone</strong> (ReferenceError before declaration), no re-declaration. <code>const</code> prevents reassignment, not mutation of the object.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-2"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q2</span><span class="qtext">Hoisting.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Declarations are moved to the top of their scope at compile time. Function declarations are fully hoisted; function expressions and arrow functions are not.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-3"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q3</span><span class="qtext">Closures.</span></p><div class="a"><p>A function retains access to its lexical scope after the outer function returns.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">function</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">counter</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">let</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">c</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mf">0</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">++</span><span class="nx">c</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="kd">const</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">next</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">counter</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">next</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="c1">// 1</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Uses: data privacy, partial application, memoisation, <code>useState</code> internals, the classic loop-with-<code>var</code> bug (fixed by <code>let</code>).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-4"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q4</span><span class="qtext"><code>this</code> binding rules.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Default (global/undefined in strict) → implicit (<code>obj.fn()</code>) → explicit (<code>call</code>/<code>apply</code>/<code>bind</code>) → <code>new</code> → arrow functions have <strong>no own <code>this</code></strong>; they inherit from the enclosing lexical scope (which is why they're used for callbacks and class fields).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-5"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q5</span><span class="qtext"><code>==</code> vs <code>===</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>==</code> coerces types, <code>===</code> doesn't. Always use <code>===</code>. Know the falsy list: <code>false, 0, -0, 0n, "", null, undefined, NaN</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-6"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q6</span><span class="qtext"><code>null</code> vs <code>undefined</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>undefined</code> = declared but not assigned (or missing); <code>null</code> = deliberate absence. <code>typeof null === "object"</code> is a historic bug.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-7"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q7</span><span class="qtext">Shallow vs deep copy in JS.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>{...obj}</code> / <code>Object.assign</code> are shallow. Deep: <code>structuredClone(obj)</code> (modern, handles Dates/Maps/cycles) or <code>JSON.parse(JSON.stringify())</code> (loses functions, <code>undefined</code>, Dates become strings).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-8"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q8</span><span class="qtext">The event loop.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Call stack → Web APIs → <strong>microtask queue</strong> (Promises, <code>queueMicrotask</code>, <code>MutationObserver</code>) drained completely → then one <strong>macrotask</strong> (setTimeout, setInterval, I/O, UI events) → repeat. Microtasks always beat macrotasks.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nx">console</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">log</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mf">1</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="nx">setTimeout</span><span class="p">(()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">console</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">log</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mf">2</span><span class="p">),</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mf">0</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="nb">Promise</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">resolve</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="nx">then</span><span class="p">(()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">console</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">log</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mf">3</span><span class="p">));</span>
<span class="nx">console</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">log</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mf">4</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="c1">// 1 4 3 2</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-9"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q9</span><span class="qtext">Callbacks → Promises → async/await.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Promise states: pending → fulfilled/rejected (settled, immutable). <code>Promise.all</code> (fails fast), <code>allSettled</code> (never rejects), <code>race</code> (first settled), <code>any</code> (first fulfilled). <code>async</code> always returns a Promise; <code>await</code> unwraps it. Error handling with <code>try/catch</code>, and don't forget unhandled rejections.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">const</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">[</span><span class="nx">user</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">orders</span><span class="p">]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">await</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nb">Promise</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">all</span><span class="p">([</span><span class="nx">fetchUser</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">id</span><span class="p">),</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">fetchOrders</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">id</span><span class="p">)]);</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-10"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q10</span><span class="qtext"><code>map</code> vs <code>forEach</code> vs <code>filter</code> vs <code>reduce</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>map</code> returns a new mapped array; <code>forEach</code> returns undefined (side effects); <code>filter</code> returns a subset; <code>reduce</code> folds to a single value.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-11"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q11</span><span class="qtext">Debounce vs throttle.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Debounce = run only after X ms of silence (search-as-you-type). Throttle = run at most once per X ms (scroll, resize).</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">const</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">debounce</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">fn</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">ms</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">let</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">t</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(...</span><span class="nx">a</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">clearTimeout</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">t</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">t</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">setTimeout</span><span class="p">(()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">fn</span><span class="p">(...</span><span class="nx">a</span><span class="p">),</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">ms</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">};</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">};</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-12"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q12</span><span class="qtext">Prototypal inheritance.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Objects link to a prototype via <code>[[Prototype]]</code>; lookups walk the chain. <code>class</code> is syntactic sugar over it.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-13"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q13</span><span class="qtext">ES6+ features.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Arrow functions, destructuring, spread/rest, template literals, default params, modules (<code>import/export</code>), classes, <code>Map</code>/<code>Set</code>, <code>Symbol</code>, generators, optional chaining <code>?.</code>, nullish coalescing <code>??</code>, <code>Object.entries/fromEntries</code>, <code>Array.at</code>, top-level await.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-14"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q14</span><span class="qtext"><code>localStorage</code> vs <code>sessionStorage</code> vs cookies.</span></p><div class="a"><p>localStorage persists, ~5–10MB, not sent to server; sessionStorage per tab; cookies small, sent with every request, support <code>HttpOnly</code>/<code>Secure</code>/<code>SameSite</code> (the right place for auth tokens).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-15"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q15</span><span class="qtext">CORS — explain it.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Browsers block cross-origin requests unless the server responds with <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> etc. Non-simple requests trigger a <strong>preflight</strong> <code>OPTIONS</code>. With credentials you can't use <code>*</code>. In Spring: <code>@CrossOrigin</code> or a <code>CorsConfigurationSource</code> bean.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-16"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q16</span><span class="qtext">XSS and CSRF.</span></p><div class="a"><p>XSS = injected script executes in the user's context; prevent with output encoding, avoid <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code>/<code>innerHTML</code>, CSP headers, sanitise (DOMPurify). CSRF = the browser sends cookies automatically on a forged request; prevent with anti-CSRF tokens + <code>SameSite=Lax/Strict</code>. Note: token-in-header auth is not CSRF-vulnerable, cookie auth is.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p4-2-typescript">2. TypeScript<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p4-2-typescript">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q4-17"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q17</span><span class="qtext">Why TypeScript?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Compile-time type safety, better IDE support/refactoring, self-documenting contracts, catches whole classes of bugs before runtime. Types are erased at compile time.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-18"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q18</span><span class="qtext"><code>interface</code> vs <code>type</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Interfaces are extendable and support declaration merging — good for object shapes and public APIs. <code>type</code> aliases handle unions, intersections, tuples, mapped and conditional types.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-19"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q19</span><span class="qtext">Key features.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Generics, union/intersection types, literal types, <code>enum</code> vs <code>as const</code>, <code>unknown</code> vs <code>any</code> vs <code>never</code>, type guards and narrowing, discriminated unions, utility types (<code>Partial</code>, <code>Required</code>, <code>Pick</code>, <code>Omit</code>, <code>Record</code>, <code>Readonly</code>, <code>ReturnType</code>, <code>Awaited</code>), optional chaining, strict null checks, decorators (Angular relies on these).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-20"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q20</span><span class="qtext"><code>any</code> vs <code>unknown</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>any</code> disables checking; <code>unknown</code> is the safe top type — you must narrow before use. Prefer <code>unknown</code>.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p4-3-react">3. React<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p4-3-react">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q4-21"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q21</span><span class="qtext">What is the virtual DOM and reconciliation?</span></p><div class="a"><p>React keeps an in-memory tree; on state change it diffs the new tree against the old and applies the minimal set of real DOM mutations. Heuristics: different element types → replace the subtree; lists use <strong>keys</strong> to match children.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-22"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q22</span><span class="qtext">Why do keys matter, and why not the array index?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Keys identify elements across renders. Index keys break when the list is reordered/filtered/inserted-into — React reuses the wrong DOM node, so component state and inputs get attached to the wrong row. Use a stable unique ID.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-23"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q23</span><span class="qtext">Class vs function components / why hooks?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Hooks let function components hold state and side effects, make logic reusable via custom hooks (instead of HOCs/render props), avoid <code>this</code> binding, and reduce boilerplate.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-24"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q24</span><span class="qtext">Rules of hooks and why.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Only at the top level (never in conditions/loops) and only in components or custom hooks. React tracks hooks by <strong>call order</strong>, so a conditional hook shifts the indices and corrupts state.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-25"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q25</span><span class="qtext"><code>useState</code> — what's the functional update form for?</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nx">setCount</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">c</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">c</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mf">1</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// correct when the next value depends on the previous</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Because state updates are asynchronous and batched, <code>setCount(count + 1)</code> twice in a row only increments once.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-26"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q26</span><span class="qtext"><code>useEffect</code> — dependency array semantics and cleanup.</span></p><div class="a"><p>No array → after every render. <code>[]</code> → once on mount. <code>[a, b]</code> → when those change. Return a cleanup function that runs before the next effect and on unmount (unsubscribe, clear timers, abort fetches).</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nx">useEffect</span><span class="p">(()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="kd">const</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">ctrl</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="ow">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">AbortController</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="nx">fetch</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">url</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">signal</span><span class="o">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">ctrl</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">signal</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}).</span><span class="nx">then</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">r</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">r</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">json</span><span class="p">()).</span><span class="nx">then</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">setData</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">ctrl</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">abort</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w">          </span><span class="c1">// prevents state-update-after-unmount + race conditions</span>
<span class="p">},</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">[</span><span class="nx">url</span><span class="p">]);</span>
</code></pre></div><p>In React 18 StrictMode dev, effects run twice on mount deliberately — to surface missing cleanup.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-27"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q27</span><span class="qtext"><code>useMemo</code> vs <code>useCallback</code> vs <code>React.memo</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>useMemo</code> caches a computed <strong>value</strong>; <code>useCallback</code> caches a <strong>function identity</strong>; <code>React.memo</code> skips re-rendering a component when props are shallow-equal. <code>useCallback</code> is only useful when the callback is passed to a memoised child or is a hook dependency. Don't memoise everything — it has its own cost.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-28"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q28</span><span class="qtext"><code>useRef</code> — two uses.</span></p><div class="a"><p>A mutable box that persists across renders <strong>without triggering a re-render</strong> (timer IDs, previous values), and DOM access (<code>ref={inputRef}</code> → <code>inputRef.current.focus()</code>).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-29"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q29</span><span class="qtext"><code>useContext</code> — and its performance trap.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Avoids prop drilling. But <strong>every consumer re-renders when any part of the context value changes</strong> — so split contexts by concern, memoise the value object, or use a state library for high-frequency state.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-30"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q30</span><span class="qtext"><code>useReducer</code> vs <code>useState</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Reducer for complex state with multiple sub-values or when the next state depends on the previous in non-trivial ways; also makes transitions testable in isolation.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-31"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q31</span><span class="qtext">Custom hooks — give an example.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">function</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">useDebouncedValue</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">value</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">delay</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mf">300</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="kd">const</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">[</span><span class="nx">v</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">setV</span><span class="p">]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">useState</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">value</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="nx">useEffect</span><span class="p">(()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">const</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">t</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">setTimeout</span><span class="p">(()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">setV</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">value</span><span class="p">),</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">delay</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">clearTimeout</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">t</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">},</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">[</span><span class="nx">value</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">delay</span><span class="p">]);</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">v</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-32"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q32</span><span class="qtext">Controlled vs uncontrolled components.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Controlled = value comes from React state (<code>value</code> + <code>onChange</code>) — single source of truth, easy validation. Uncontrolled = the DOM holds it, read via a ref — less code, used by React Hook Form for performance.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-33"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q33</span><span class="qtext">Lifting state up / prop drilling / composition.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Move shared state to the nearest common ancestor; avoid deep drilling via context, composition (<code>children</code>), or a store.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-34"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q34</span><span class="qtext">State management options.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Local state → Context → Redux Toolkit (predictable, devtools, middleware; RTK removes most boilerplate) → Zustand/Jotai (lighter) → <strong>React Query / TanStack Query</strong> for <em>server</em> state (caching, background refetch, stale-while-revalidate, retries, dedupe). Key insight to state in an interview: <strong>server state and client state are different problems</strong> — using Redux for server data is the most common over-engineering mistake.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-35"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q35</span><span class="qtext">Redux flow.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Store → dispatch(action) → reducer (pure, immutable) → new state → subscribed components re-render. Middleware (thunk/saga) for async. RTK's <code>createSlice</code> + <code>createAsyncThunk</code> + Immer for immutable-looking mutations.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-36"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q36</span><span class="qtext">How do you optimise a slow React app?</span></p><div class="a"><p>React DevTools Profiler first. Then: memoise expensive subtrees, stabilise props (<code>useCallback</code>/<code>useMemo</code>), virtualise long lists (<code>react-window</code>), code-split routes with <code>React.lazy</code> + <code>Suspense</code>, avoid inline object/array literals as props, keep context values narrow, debounce inputs, move state down so fewer components re-render, and check for unnecessary top-level state.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-37"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q37</span><span class="qtext">Error boundaries.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Class components with <code>componentDidCatch</code>/<code>getDerivedStateFromError</code> catch render-time errors in the subtree. They do <strong>not</strong> catch event handlers, async code, or SSR errors.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-38"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q38</span><span class="qtext">React 18 features.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Automatic batching everywhere, <code>startTransition</code>/<code>useTransition</code> for non-urgent updates, <code>useDeferredValue</code>, Suspense for data fetching, concurrent rendering, <code>useId</code>. React 19: Actions, <code>use()</code>, server components (know the concept: rendered on the server, zero client JS, can access the backend directly).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-39"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q39</span><span class="qtext">How do you test React?</span></p><div class="a"><p>React Testing Library (query by role/text like a user, not by implementation), Jest/Vitest, <code>userEvent</code>, MSW to mock the network, Cypress/Playwright for E2E.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p4-4-angular">4. Angular<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p4-4-angular">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q4-40"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q40</span><span class="qtext">Angular vs React — the honest comparison.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Angular is a full opinionated framework (DI, router, forms, HTTP, RxJS, CLI, TypeScript-first) — great for large enterprise apps with consistent structure. React is a library; you assemble the ecosystem. Angular uses real DOM with change detection + Ivy; React uses a virtual DOM. Angular's learning curve is steeper but conventions are enforced.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-41"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q41</span><span class="qtext">Angular building blocks.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Modules (<code>NgModule</code>) or <strong>standalone components</strong> (v14+, now the default direction), components, templates, directives (structural <code>*ngIf</code>/<code>*ngFor</code>, attribute <code>ngClass</code>/<code>ngStyle</code>, custom), pipes (pure/impure, <code>async</code>), services, DI, routing.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-42"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q42</span><span class="qtext">Component lifecycle hooks in order.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>ngOnChanges</code> → <code>ngOnInit</code> → <code>ngDoCheck</code> → <code>ngAfterContentInit</code> → <code>ngAfterContentChecked</code> → <code>ngAfterViewInit</code> → <code>ngAfterViewChecked</code> → <code>ngOnDestroy</code>.
<code>ngOnChanges</code> fires on <code>@Input</code> changes; <code>ngOnInit</code> once after the first <code>ngOnChanges</code>; <code>ngOnDestroy</code> is where you unsubscribe.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-43"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q43</span><span class="qtext">Data binding types.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Interpolation <code>{{ }}</code>, property <code>[prop]</code>, event <code>(click)</code>, two-way <code>[(ngModel)]</code> (banana-in-a-box = property + event sugar).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-44"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q44</span><span class="qtext"><code>@Input</code> / <code>@Output</code> / EventEmitter.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Parent→child via <code>@Input</code>, child→parent via <code>@Output</code> <code>EventEmitter</code> + <code>.emit()</code>. Sibling communication via a shared service with a <code>Subject</code>/<code>BehaviorSubject</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-45"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q45</span><span class="qtext">Change detection — Default vs OnPush.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Default: on any event, Angular checks the whole component tree (Zone.js patches async APIs to trigger it). <strong>OnPush</strong>: the component is checked only when an <code>@Input</code> reference changes, an event originates in it, an <code>async</code> pipe emits, or you call <code>markForCheck()</code>. OnPush + immutable data is the main Angular performance lever. Also: <code>trackBy</code> on <code>*ngFor</code>, <code>detach()</code>/<code>runOutsideAngular()</code> for hot loops. Angular 16+ <strong>signals</strong> aim to remove Zone.js entirely (fine-grained reactivity).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-46"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q46</span><span class="qtext">Angular DI — providers and hierarchy.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>@Injectable({providedIn: 'root'})</code> gives an app-wide singleton and is tree-shakeable. Providers can also live on a module (module scope) or a component (a new instance per component instance). Injector hierarchy: element injector → module injector → root. Tokens: class, <code>InjectionToken</code> for non-class values, <code>useClass</code>/<code>useValue</code>/<code>useFactory</code>/<code>useExisting</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-47"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q47</span><span class="qtext">RxJS — Observable vs Promise.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Observables are lazy, cancellable, can emit multiple values, and compose with operators. Promises are eager, single-value, uncancellable.
Subjects: <code>Subject</code> (no initial, no replay), <code>BehaviorSubject</code> (needs initial value, replays last — the usual choice for state), <code>ReplaySubject(n)</code>, <code>AsyncSubject</code>.
<strong>Operators you must know:</strong> <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>tap</code>, <code>switchMap</code> (cancels the previous — correct for typeahead search), <code>mergeMap</code> (concurrent), <code>concatMap</code> (sequential, preserves order — correct for writes), <code>exhaustMap</code> (ignores new while busy — correct for a submit button), <code>debounceTime</code>, <code>distinctUntilChanged</code>, <code>combineLatest</code>, <code>forkJoin</code> (like Promise.all), <code>withLatestFrom</code>, <code>catchError</code>, <code>retry</code>/<code>retryWhen</code>, <code>shareReplay</code>, <code>takeUntil</code>, <code>startWith</code>, <code>finalize</code>.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="k">this</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">results$</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">this</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">searchControl</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">valueChanges</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">pipe</span><span class="p">(</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="nx">debounceTime</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mf">300</span><span class="p">),</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="nx">distinctUntilChanged</span><span class="p">(),</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="nx">switchMap</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">term</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">this</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">api</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">search</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">term</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="nx">pipe</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">catchError</span><span class="p">(()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">of</span><span class="p">([])))),</span>
<span class="p">);</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-48"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q48</span><span class="qtext">How do you avoid memory leaks from subscriptions?</span></p><div class="a"><p>The <code>async</code> pipe (auto-unsubscribes — best), <code>takeUntil(this.destroy$)</code> with a <code>Subject</code> completed in <code>ngOnDestroy</code>, <code>takeUntilDestroyed()</code> (v16+), or manual <code>Subscription.unsubscribe()</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-49"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q49</span><span class="qtext">Template-driven vs reactive forms.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Template-driven: <code>ngModel</code>, simple, async validation is awkward, logic in the template. <strong>Reactive</strong>: <code>FormGroup</code>/<code>FormControl</code>/<code>FormArray</code> defined in TS, synchronous access, easy dynamic forms and custom validators, testable. Prefer reactive for anything non-trivial.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nx">form</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">this</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">fb</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">group</span><span class="p">({</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="nx">email</span><span class="o">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">[</span><span class="s1">''</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">[</span><span class="nx">Validators</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">required</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">Validators</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">email</span><span class="p">]],</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="nx">stake</span><span class="o">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">[</span><span class="kc">null</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">[</span><span class="nx">Validators</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">required</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">Validators</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">min</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mf">0.1</span><span class="p">)]],</span>
<span class="p">});</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-50"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q50</span><span class="qtext">Routing.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>RouterModule.forRoot(routes)</code>, route params (<code>ActivatedRoute.paramMap</code>), query params, child routes, <strong>lazy loading</strong> (<code>loadChildren: () =&gt; import('./x').then(m =&gt; m.XModule)</code> or <code>loadComponent</code>), guards (<code>CanActivate</code>, <code>CanDeactivate</code>, <code>CanMatch</code>, <code>Resolve</code>), preloading strategies.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-51"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q51</span><span class="qtext">Interceptors.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>HttpInterceptor</code> for attaching auth tokens, logging, global error handling, retry, loading spinners.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nx">intercept</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">req</span><span class="o">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">HttpRequest</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="nx">any</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">next</span><span class="o">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">HttpHandler</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="kd">const</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">cloned</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">req</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">clone</span><span class="p">({</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">setHeaders</span><span class="o">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">Authorization</span><span class="o">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="sb">`Bearer </span><span class="si">${</span><span class="k">this</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">auth</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">token</span><span class="si">}</span><span class="sb">`</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">});</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">next</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">handle</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">cloned</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="nx">pipe</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">catchError</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">err</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">if</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nx">err</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">status</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">===</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mf">401</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">this</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">auth</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">logout</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">throwError</span><span class="p">(()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">=&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nx">err</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}));</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-52"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q52</span><span class="qtext">Pipes.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Pure (recalculated only on reference change — default) vs impure (every CD cycle — expensive, e.g. the built-in <code>async</code> pipe is impure). Custom pipe via <code>@Pipe</code> + <code>transform()</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-53"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q53</span><span class="qtext">ViewChild/ContentChild, ng-content, ng-template/ng-container.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>@ViewChild</code> accesses elements/components in your own template (available in <code>ngAfterViewInit</code>); <code>@ContentChild</code> accesses projected content. <code>&lt;ng-content&gt;</code> is content projection (like React <code>children</code>). <code>ng-container</code> groups without adding DOM.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-54"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q54</span><span class="qtext">What are Angular signals (v16+)?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Fine-grained reactive primitives: <code>signal()</code>, <code>computed()</code>, <code>effect()</code>. They let Angular know exactly what changed, enabling zoneless change detection and far fewer checks than the Zone.js tree walk.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-55"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q55</span><span class="qtext">AOT vs JIT; what is Ivy?</span></p><div class="a"><p>AOT compiles templates at build time — smaller bundle, faster startup, template errors caught at build (default in prod). Ivy is the rendering engine enabling tree-shaking, smaller bundles, and better debugging.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-56"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q56</span><span class="qtext">NgRx (if they ask about state).</span></p><div class="a"><p>Store, Actions, Reducers, Selectors (memoised), Effects (side effects with RxJS), Entity adapter. Same Redux mental model. Use only when the state is genuinely shared and complex.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-57"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q57</span><span class="qtext">Testing Angular.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Jasmine/Karma or Jest, <code>TestBed</code>, <code>ComponentFixture</code>, <code>HttpTestingController</code>, <code>fakeAsync</code>/<code>tick</code>, spies via <code>jasmine.createSpyObj</code>.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p4-5-full-stack-integration-epam-will-ask-this">5. Full-stack integration (EPAM will ask this)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p4-5-full-stack-integration-epam-will-ask-this">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q4-58"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q58</span><span class="qtext">How do the frontend and backend communicate in your app?</span></p><div class="a"><p>REST over HTTPS with JSON DTOs; auth via JWT bearer token in the <code>Authorization</code> header (or an httpOnly cookie); CORS configured for known origins; a shared OpenAPI/Swagger contract used to generate the TS client so types stay in sync; errors as RFC 7807 problem details rendered into user-friendly messages; a correlation ID header propagated for tracing.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-59"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q59</span><span class="qtext">How do you handle file upload / long-running requests?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Multipart upload with a size limit, or pre-signed S3 URLs so the file never touches your service. For long jobs: <code>202 Accepted</code> + a job ID, then poll or push via WebSocket/SSE.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-60"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q60</span><span class="qtext">Real-time updates?</span></p><div class="a"><p>WebSocket (STOMP over SockJS in Spring, or raw), SSE for one-way, or polling as a fallback. Discuss scaling: sticky sessions or an external registry, and a message broker for fan-out.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-61"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q61</span><span class="qtext">How do you handle pagination end to end?</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>?page=0&amp;size=20&amp;sort=createdAt,desc</code> → Spring <code>Pageable</code> → <code>Page&lt;T&gt;</code> with <code>content</code>, <code>totalElements</code>, <code>totalPages</code>. For very large offsets use keyset/cursor pagination (<code>?after=&lt;lastId&gt;</code>), which stays O(1).</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p4-6-devops-docker-kubernetes-ci-cd">6. DevOps, Docker, Kubernetes, CI/CD<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p4-6-devops-docker-kubernetes-ci-cd">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q4-62"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q62</span><span class="qtext">Docker basics.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Image vs container, layers and caching, <code>Dockerfile</code> instructions, volumes, networks, <code>docker-compose</code>.
<strong>Multi-stage build for a Spring Boot app:</strong></p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="k">FROM</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-21</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">AS</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">build</span>
<span class="k">WORKDIR</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">/app</span>
<span class="k">COPY</span><span class="w"> </span>pom.xml<span class="w"> </span>.
<span class="k">RUN</span><span class="w"> </span>mvn<span class="w"> </span>-B<span class="w"> </span>dependency:go-offline<span class="w">      </span>#<span class="w"> </span>cache<span class="w"> </span>layer
<span class="k">COPY</span><span class="w"> </span>src<span class="w"> </span>./src
<span class="k">RUN</span><span class="w"> </span>mvn<span class="w"> </span>-B<span class="w"> </span>clean<span class="w"> </span>package<span class="w"> </span>-DskipTests

<span class="k">FROM</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">eclipse-temurin:21-jre-alpine</span>
<span class="k">WORKDIR</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">/app</span>
<span class="k">RUN</span><span class="w"> </span>addgroup<span class="w"> </span>-S<span class="w"> </span>app<span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&amp;&amp;</span><span class="w"> </span>adduser<span class="w"> </span>-S<span class="w"> </span>app<span class="w"> </span>-G<span class="w"> </span>app
<span class="k">COPY</span><span class="w"> </span>--from<span class="o">=</span>build<span class="w"> </span>/app/target/*.jar<span class="w"> </span>app.jar
<span class="k">USER</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">app</span>
<span class="k">EXPOSE</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">8080</span>
<span class="k">ENTRYPOINT</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">[</span><span class="s2">"java"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="s2">"-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="s2">"-jar"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="s2">"app.jar"</span><span class="p">]</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Best practices: small base image, non-root user, <code>.dockerignore</code>, pin versions, order layers by change frequency, one process per container, health checks, no secrets baked in.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-63"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q63</span><span class="qtext">JVM in containers.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Modern JVMs are container-aware (<code>UseContainerSupport</code>); prefer <code>-XX:MaxRAMPercentage</code> over <code>-Xmx</code> so it scales with the pod limit. Beware CPU limits throttling GC threads.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-64"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q64</span><span class="qtext">Kubernetes concepts.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Pod, ReplicaSet, Deployment (rolling update, <code>maxSurge</code>/<code>maxUnavailable</code>), Service (ClusterIP/NodePort/LoadBalancer), Ingress, ConfigMap, Secret, HPA, StatefulSet, DaemonSet, Job/CronJob, namespaces, resource requests/limits, liveness vs readiness vs startup probes (map these to Actuator health groups), PDB, node affinity.
<strong>Readiness vs liveness</strong> is a favourite: readiness removes the pod from the Service when it can't serve; liveness restarts it. Getting liveness wrong causes restart loops under load.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-65"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q65</span><span class="qtext">CI/CD pipeline you've built.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Commit → build + unit tests → static analysis (Sonar) + dependency scan → package → build/push image → deploy to dev → integration/contract tests → deploy to staging → smoke tests → manual approval → prod (blue/green or canary) → post-deploy monitoring with automatic rollback on SLO breach. Tools: Jenkins/GitHub Actions/GitLab CI, Nexus/Artifactory, ArgoCD or Helm.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-66"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q66</span><span class="qtext">Deployment strategies.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Rolling, Blue/Green (instant rollback, double resources), Canary (gradual with metric gates), Feature flags (decouple deploy from release — mention this, it's a mature answer).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-67"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q67</span><span class="qtext">Git workflow.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Trunk-based with short-lived feature branches and feature flags, or GitFlow for release-train products. Know: <code>rebase</code> vs <code>merge</code> (rebase for a clean local history, never rebase shared branches), <code>cherry-pick</code>, <code>stash</code>, <code>revert</code> vs <code>reset</code>, resolving conflicts, squash merges, conventional commits, and how you'd revert a bad release.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-68"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q68</span><span class="qtext">Twelve-factor app.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Codebase, dependencies, config in env, backing services as attached resources, build/release/run separation, stateless processes, port binding, concurrency, disposability, dev/prod parity, logs as event streams, admin processes as one-off jobs.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-69"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q69</span><span class="qtext">How do you manage secrets?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Never in Git. Vault / AWS Secrets Manager / K8s Secrets (backed by KMS) / Spring Cloud Config with encryption, injected as env vars or mounted files, with rotation.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q4-70"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q70</span><span class="qtext">Cloud services you should be able to name (AWS flavour).</span></p><div class="a"><p>EC2, ECS/EKS, Lambda, S3, RDS/Aurora, DynamoDB, ElastiCache, SQS/SNS, MSK (Kafka), API Gateway, CloudFront, Route53, IAM roles, CloudWatch, Secrets Manager, ALB. Have one story about how you used at least three of these together.</p></div></section><hr/>
<p><em>Next: Part 5 — Behavioural, company-specific prep, and a study plan.</em></p></div></section>
<section class="panel" id="panel-5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-5" hidden><header class="part-head"><span class="part-n">Part 05</span><h1>Behavioural &amp; Company Prep</h1><p class="kicker">Stories, plan, tactics <span class="dot">·</span> 0 questions</p></header><div class="prose">
<hr/>
<h2 id="p5-1-what-each-company-is-actually-assessing">1. What each company is actually assessing<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p5-1-what-each-company-is-actually-assessing">#</a></h2>
<h3 id="p5-epam-full-stack-80-backend-20-react-angular">EPAM — Full Stack (80% backend / 20% React + Angular)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p5-epam-full-stack-80-backend-20-react-angular">#</a></h3>
<p>EPAM is a consultancy, so round 2 typically checks:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Breadth over depth in places</strong> — they staff you on client projects, so they want to know you can pick things up. They will ask about <em>both</em> React and Angular.</li>
<li><strong>Core Java depth</strong> — collections internals, concurrency, JVM. EPAM's technical interviews are known for going deep on fundamentals ("how does HashMap resize?", "explain the JMM").</li>
<li><strong>Clean code, SOLID, design patterns</strong> — EPAM interviewers routinely ask "which patterns have you used and why".</li>
<li><strong>Live coding</strong> — expect to write code on a shared editor. Data structures, streams, or a small design (e.g. "model a parking lot / library system with classes").</li>
<li><strong>Communication in English</strong> — they place you client-facing. Speak in structured, complete answers.</li>
<li><strong>Your project architecture</strong> — be able to draw your current system on a whiteboard in 3 minutes.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Likely EPAM-specific asks:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>"Design the class model for X" (LLD: parking lot, elevator, vending machine, library, ATM, splitwise, chess). Practise: identify entities → interfaces → apply a pattern (Strategy for pricing, State for status, Factory for creation) → discuss extensibility.</li>
<li>"How would you convert this Angular component to React?" / "Where would you put business logic in each?"</li>
<li>"How do you make sure your code is testable?"</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="p5-entain-java-backend">Entain — Java Backend<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p5-entain-java-backend">#</a></h3>
<p>Entain (Ladbrokes, Coral, bwin, Sportingbet, BetMGM) is a <strong>high-traffic, regulated betting/gaming</strong> business. Round 2 typically checks:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Concurrency and correctness under load</strong> — money and bets, so double-spend, idempotency, locking.</li>
<li><strong>Spring Boot + microservices + Kafka</strong> in anger.</li>
<li><strong>Latency awareness</strong> — caching, DB tuning, GC pauses.</li>
<li><strong>Data consistency</strong> — transactions, isolation levels, distributed transactions/saga.</li>
<li><strong>Domain curiosity</strong> — knowing what odds, markets, selections, stakes, settlement, cash-out and accumulators are will genuinely impress. So will mentioning <strong>responsible gambling</strong> and <strong>regulatory/audit requirements</strong> unprompted.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Likely Entain-specific asks:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>"A user places two bets simultaneously and the wallet only has enough for one. What happens?"</li>
<li>"How do you guarantee a bet isn't placed twice when the client retries?"</li>
<li>"Odds change between the user seeing them and submitting. Handle it."</li>
<li>"Design the settlement flow after a match finishes."</li>
<li>"How would you handle 50× traffic at kickoff?"</li>
<li>"How do you make a Kafka consumer idempotent?"</li>
</ul>
