<h2 id="p5-7-interview-day-tactics">7. Interview-day tactics<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p5-7-interview-day-tactics">#</a></h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Think aloud.</strong> For coding, state your approach and complexity <em>before</em> writing. Ask about constraints and edge cases (nulls, empty input, duplicates, overflow, concurrency).</li>
<li><strong>Never bluff.</strong> "I haven't used that in production, but my understanding is X — how do you use it here?" scores far better than a confident wrong answer. Interviewers at both companies will dig until you're at your limit; hitting your limit gracefully is expected and fine.</li>
<li><strong>Answer in layers.</strong> Give the direct answer in one sentence, then the detail, then the trade-off. Don't monologue for four minutes on question one.</li>
<li><strong>Anchor to experience.</strong> "In my current project we hit exactly this — we..." is worth more than textbook recall.</li>
<li><strong>Always mention trade-offs.</strong> At 5+ years they're testing judgement, not recall. Every design answer should end with "the cost of this is…".</li>
<li><strong>If you blank:</strong> say "let me think for a second", restate the question, and reason from first principles out loud. Silence is worse than visible reasoning.</li>
<li><strong>For system design:</strong> clarify before designing, and manage the clock — don't spend 15 minutes on the API contract.</li>
<li><strong>Have your setup ready:</strong> stable connection, quiet room, notepad and pen, a shared-editor-friendly browser, and your project diagram open.</li>
</ul>
